{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi1AjvRLtQf5Wg2LSvXP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01KjHXws4C9tXbTNUxC6WEaW","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-065619__xa7e588/work/PLAN.md","limit":470},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5139,"cache_read_input_tokens":19376,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5139},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"f6e5ef5e-f227-46b4-9f84-8d63486e0dec","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:56:30.799Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi1Ajh2ZCFCcwcveYUzw","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01KjHXws4C9tXbTNUxC6WEaW":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-065619__xa7e588/work/PLAN.md","limit":470}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KjHXws4C9tXbTNUxC6WEaW","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# THUGBY TURBO\n2\t\n3\t*Over-the-top arcade rugby sevens. Big hits, bigger hair, seven a side.*\n4\t\n5\tThis is the build plan for the developers (people or AI models) who will make the demo described in `TASK.md`. Read\n6\t`TASK.md` first, then this file. Everything the build needs is here or in the engine's handoff package. Every engine API\n7\tnamed below was checked against **my-3D2dge v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`) and\n8\tthe **AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`). Where this plan\n9\tsays \"verify\", the builder checks the detail in a spike before relying on it.\n10\t\n11\t---\n12\t\n13\t## 0. Read this first\n14\t\n15\t**The core loop, in one sentence (put it at the top of the game's source):**\n16\t*Run at their line, pass it backwards, smash through or step round them, ground it over the line and kick the\n17\tconversion, while seven lunatics in the other colours try to flatten you, and the referee looks the other way.*\n18\t\n19\t**Where things are:** the game in one page (§2); architecture (§4); controls (§6); every rule and number (§7); content\n20\t(§9); build order (§17); risks (§18); tests (§19); the done list (§21).\n21\t\n22\t**Working rules on this machine (from TASK.md):** keep every dev file in the dev subfolder; read no files outside what\n23\tthe build needs (other agents share the directory, ports and processes); prefer `file://` and the engine's headless\n24\ttools; a server (only the AI Hub harness needs one) binds `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page\n25\tis yours before trusting a result; stop only processes you started, by PID (never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`).\n26\t\n27\t**Hard requirements from TASK.md, and where this plan covers each:**\n28\t\n29\t| TASK.md asks for | Where |\n30\t|---|---|\n31\t| Title riffing on the source, picked by a real RNG | §1 |\n32\t| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §2, §5 |\n33\t| A single HTML file, a dev subfolder, the file names | §1, §4.6 |\n34\t| Built on the engine's handoff package; changes allowed | §3 |\n35\t| Native art style plus new rugby art; native animation plus new rugby animations | §10, §11 |\n36\t| All audio written in code; Animalese-style gibberish voices | §12 |\n37\t| AI Hub multiplayer, and multiplayer on its own | §15 |\n38\t| A feature-complete, polished vertical slice that shows every mechanic | §7, §8, §9 |\n39\t| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | §14 |\n40\t| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | §13 |\n41\t| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; glyphs follow the last device; Xbox option | §6, §10.6 |\n42\t| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\" plus Settings; attract still runs | §6.6 |\n43\t| Writing in the source's style | §9.3, §9.5 |\n44\t| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §21 |\n45\t| Source info and feedback in the chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen; exit-interview.json | §20 |\n46\t| Headless tests behind one command; a scripted playthrough of the whole game | §19 |\n47\t\n48\t---\n49\t\n50\t## 1. Title, names and deliverables\n51\t\n52\t**Title: THUGBY TURBO.** Picked as TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on the word \"rugby\" were brainstormed silently,\n53\tthen one was drawn with a real random number generator: Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy) returned 16,\n54\tentry #17 of 50: **\"Thugby Turbo\"**. Record this in the About screen. Do not re-pick it.\n55\t\n56\t| Thing | Name |\n57\t|---|---|\n58\t| Title on screen | THUGBY TURBO |\n59\t| GameTitle in file names | `ThugbyTurbo` |\n60\t| SOURCE in file names | `Rugby` |\n61\t| Dev subfolder (in this folder) | `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828/` |\n62\t| The deliverable (in this folder, beside PLAN.md) | `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828.html` |\n63\t| Exit interview (in this folder, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\n64\t| Page `<title>` | `Thugby Turbo` |\n65\t| Settings/save id (`new E.Game({ id })`) | `thugby-turbo` |\n66\t\n67\tThe HTML is one self-contained file: the AI Hub session client first in `<head>`, then the engine and the game inlined.\n68\tNo CDN is needed (the engine has no dependencies); do not add any.\n69\t\n70\t---\n71\t\n72\t## 2. The game in one page\n73\t\n74\t**Genre:** arcade sports, rugby sevens played for laughs and for keeps (NBA Jam's turbo and \"on fire\", Mutant League's\n75\tcartoon violence, Speedball's crowd-thrown power-ups), wrapped in a short story campaign, with couch and online party\n76\tmultiplayer for 1 to 4 players. Sevens is the real format that suits an arcade game best: seven a side on a full\n77\tpitch, short halves, open space, sudden-death extra time, and the scoring team kicks off.\n78\t\n79\t**Perspective:** a TV broadcast camera. The pitch runs left to right across the screen, seen from the stand at a 40°\n80\tpitch (a custom engine view, `'broadcast'`), panning with the ball. Heights read well (kicks, lineout jumps, the\n81\tposts), and passing across the pitch is up and down the screen. Players can switch to a high 55° camera\n82\t(`'threequarter'`) or near-overhead (`'topdown'`). Conversions and penalty kicks cut to a behind-the-kicker camera\n83\t(§5.4).\n84\t\n85\t**Tone:** a live Saturday broadcast on CHANNEL THUD. Two commentators (breathless Barry Bellows and bone-dry Ros\n86\tRucker), a whistle-happy, short-sighted referee, a gruff coach with rules for everything, rival captains who trash-talk\n87\tin gibberish with subtitles, mud, meat pies, a giant chicken mascot and a sin bin that is an actual bin. Affectionate,\n88\tloud, never mean.\n89\t\n90\t**What makes it rugby (all in the demo):** passing only backwards (L1 and R1 pass up and down the line); tackles that\n91\tend in rucks; tries you must ground, then a conversion from in line with where you scored; scrums, lineouts, kick-offs,\n92\t22 drop-outs, penalties with a choice (tap, kick at goal, kick to touch), drop goals, knock-ons and forward passes,\n93\toffside lines, advantage, yellow cards and the sin bin, the hooter that waits for the ball to go dead.\n94\t\n95\t**What makes it over the top (all in the demo):** a turbo button; big hits charged by holding the button that launch\n96\tpeople into orbit; sidesteps, spins, fends and dummies; a team HYPE meter that unlocks supers (THUNDER RUN, HURRICANE\n97\tHIT); a player who scores twice running is ON FIRE; fans throw power-ups onto the pitch (meat pie, iron shoulders, mega\n98\tboot, grease jersey, air horn); each stadium has a hazard (mud, a chicken pitch invader, flame jets, lightning); the\n99\treferee only calls foul play he can see, and he can be flattened (\"REF DOWN, ANYTHING GOES!\"); instant replays of the\n100\tbiggest moments; a boss who needs two tacklers.\n101\t\n102\t**Modes:** MEGA CUP (the story: training plus four matches, about 25-35 minutes), EXHIBITION (any two teams, any\n103\tstadium, 1-4 local players, versus or co-op), TRAINING GROUND (nine drills, each also a tutorial), RECORDS (arcade\n104\ttables with initials), SETTINGS, ABOUT & CREDITS. Online: AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2-4 players (§15.2).\n105\t\n106\t---\n107\t\n108\t## 3. Tech stack and the engine\n109\t\n110\t**Base:** the my-3D2dge handoff package v0.85.2, full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, 1.2 MB, readable, every module inside).\n111\tIt is a 2D engine with a 3D world: x east, y south, z up, 16 world units a tile; characters are procedural 3D skeletons\n112\t(`E.Humanoid`) drawn as pixel art; `res: 'ps1'` (320x240) with `expand: true` fills any screen (about 427x240 on 16:9).\n113\tCanvas 2D and Web Audio only. No other libraries.\n114\t\n115\t**Research done for this plan** (the builder may cite it under Research, adding its own): the handoff package's\n116\t`README.md`, `API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `kits/README.md`, `kits/sports.js`, parts of `kits/brawler.js`, the template\n117\t`game/`, `tools/check.mjs`, the headers of `modules/story.js` and `modules/arcade.js`, and the engine source around\n118\tsettings, the shell, input labels, half keyboards, dialogs and actions; the AI Hub `lib/hub/README.md`, `retrofit.md`,\n119\t`session.js` and `examples/arena.html` (the shared-world pattern).\n120\t\n121\t**Read before writing code (in this order):** `API.md` whole; `kits/sports.js` whole (the closest kit: a ball as an\n122\t`E.Body`, passes led to the receiver, `E.arc` lobs, formations, a match clock, `allowJoin`, a radio commentator, a\n123\tdeterministic `run()` proof and a `bot()`); `kits/brawler.js` around its knockdown code (`knock`, `getUp`, launch arcs);\n124\t`AI_GUIDE.md` sections Standard features, Frame order, Players, Humanoid, Audio, Shell, Testing without a browser,\n125\tRecorded sessions, Sports recipe, Building a big game; `docs/ASSETS.md` before drawing any prop; `modules/story.js` and\n126\t`modules/arcade.js` headers. Search `API-INDEX.md` for names; do not read it whole.\n127\t\n128\t**Engine features this game uses:** scenes and `game.go` transitions; `TileMap` (the pitch, stands, boards) with\n129\t`floorTex`, `cut` walls, floor tags; `E.Body`; `E.Humanoid` and `rig.drawPortrait`; `E.ACTIONS` / `E.Action` (paired\n130\tanimations: the tackle is a takedown's cousin); `E.MOVES` and custom attack specs; `E.arc`; `E.sees` and `r.sightCone`\n131\t(the referee's eyes); `r.marker` (off-screen arrows); `r.pick` and `game.mouseGround` (mouse in the world);\n132\tparticles, `game.hitFx`, `freeze`, `shake`, `flash`; canvas lighting; `game.weather`; `game.tween`, `game.script`,\n133\t`E.until`; `E.Dialog` (commentary radio, `modal: false`), `E.Story` (cutscenes as data), `E.Menu`; `E.Arcade` (attract\n134\tcycle, records with initials, CONTINUE countdown); `game.settings` (own settings), the standard pause, settings and\n135\tcontrols screens (`menu: { items, settings, controls, quit }`); `game.saves`; `game.allowJoin` / `addPlayer` (local\n136\tmultiplayer); `game.audio` music (named layers), `sting`, `define`, `sfx`; `game.debug`, `game.advance`,\n137\t`input.tap/hold/press/feed` (tests); `E.rng`.\n138\t\n139\t**Facts found during research that the build must respect:**\n140\t- The pixel font has no `✕ ○ □ △` glyphs: `input.label('x')` returns `'✕'` for a PlayStation pad, which the canvas\n141\t  font draws as `?`. Fix in the engine font (patch 1) and draw coloured button badges for prompts anyway (§10.6).\n142\t- The standard setting `glyphs` (Controller group) defaults to `'auto'`. TASK.md wants PlayStation by default: override\n143\t  it from the game (§6.4). Its `apply` only touches `game.input`; apply it to every player and to joiners.\n144\t- `Input.halfKeys` gives two keyboard players fixed button lists (F G H R T Y V B / . / , L ; ' K M) in the order of the\n145\t  preset's actions. Our layouts (§6.3) need patch 2.\n146\t- `E.Menu` has no font scale; 10-foot menus need patch 3 (or a small BigMenu of our own).\n147\t- `E.Dialog` blips one sound every second letter; Animalese needs a per-letter hook (patch 4).\n148\t- While `game.paused`, or during hit-stop (`game.freeze`), the scene's `update` does not run (only input ticks); at\n149\t  `game.timeScale = 0` it runs with `dt` 0 (so a dev panel can still read input). In an AI Hub shared world nothing may\n150\t  stop the host's world: sessions use `pausable: false`, `autoPause: false`, `corner: false`, `menu: { quit: false }`\n151\t  and no `game.freeze` (§15.2).\n152\t- `E.VIEWS` takes custom views: `E.VIEWS.broadcast = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 40, 1, 1)`, then scenes\n153\t  name `'broadcast'`. `E.charView` draws rigs from the sprite-like angle at pitch 40 to 89.\n154\t- Headless (Node, no DOM) `new E.Game()` skips drawing; `rig.update` still costs time. Keep the simulation free of rig\n155\t  state so tests can skip rig updates (§4.4).\n156\t- The settings screen lists every settings group except Controls, Controller and Touch, so dev sliders must not be\n157\t  `game.settings` items (they would show in Settings); the dev panel is the game's own (§14).\n158\t\n159\t**Engine patches (allowed by TASK.md; keep each small, list them in `engine/PATCHES.md` and the About screen's Engine\n160\tFeedback):**\n161\t\n162\t| # | Patch | Why |\n163\t|---|---|---|\n164\t| 1 | Add `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5x7 font (`G57`) | PlayStation labels render as `?` |\n165\t| 2 | `Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` uses `preset.halves[side]` (per-action code lists) when present | two keyboard players need sensible layouts |\n166\t| 3 | `E.Menu` option `scale` (font scale, row height, box padding) | 10-foot menus |\n167\t| 4 | `E.Dialog` option/field `onLetter(ch, index, text)` called for each letter typed; `voice: null` keeps the default blip off | per-letter babble voices |\n168\t\n169\tAnything else found missing during the build (a bug, a gap) may be patched the same way and listed.\n170\t\n171\t---\n172\t\n173\t## 4. Architecture\n174\t\n175\t### 4.1 Layers and the one rule that makes everything else work\n176\t\n177\t```\n178\t Devices (pad, keys, mouse)   AI brains   Network intents   Test scripts   Attract director\n179\t              \\                   |              |                |              /\n180\t               '----------->  INTENTS (one per on-field player, per step)  <----'\n181\t                                          |\n182\t                                   MATCH SIMULATION  (rules, physics, contact, set pieces, AI-free)\n183\t                                          |\n184\t                                 MATCH STATE (plain, serialisable)  ---> replay ring buffer\n185\t                                          |                         ---> network snapshots (host)\n186\t                                   PRESENTATION  (rigs, camera, HUD, FX, audio, commentary)\n187\t```\n188\t\n189\t**The rule: the simulation reads only intents and writes only `MatchState`; presentation reads only `MatchState` and\n190\tevents.** Then the same presentation draws a live match, an instant replay (recorded states), an online client\n191\t(interpolated snapshots) and the attract demo; the same simulation runs headless for tests, as the host of an online\n192\tmatch, and for the AI Hub \"server\" seat with nothing drawn.\n193\t\n194\t### 4.2 Source layout (the dev subfolder)\n195\t\n196\t```\n197\tThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828/\n198\t  engine/my-3d2dge.js        handoff engine v0.85.2 + patches (engine/PATCHES.md lists them)\n199\t  vendor/aihub-session.js    lib/hub/session.js 1.4.0, byte-for-byte unmodified\n200\t  src/                       the game, concatenated in file-name order into one IIFE by build.mjs\n201\t    00-core.js        TT namespace, constants, TUNE registry, RNG, event bus, helpers, config flags\n202\t    01-data-teams.js  teams, kits, rosters, AI styles\n203\t    02-data-venues.js stadiums, weather, hazards, crowd, boards, skylines\n204\t    03-data-content.js power-ups, commentary lines, story nodes, drills, records, credits text\n205\t    10-pitch.js       pitch geometry, TileMap, line texture, posts, stands, boards, sin bin\n206\t    11-ball.js        ball states, flight, oval bounce, out of play\n207\t    12-players.js     player entities, stats, movement, turbo, states, control assignment\n208\t    13-anim.js        custom E.ACTIONS, attack specs, rig post-processing (carry, dive...), anim-state codes\n209\t    14-laws.js        forward pass, knock-on, offside lines, touch, scoring, fouls, advantage, cards\n210\t    15-match.js       match state machine: phases, clock, halves, hooter, restarts, sudden death, stats\n211\t    16-contact.js     tackles, big hits, dive and gang tackles, fends, sidesteps, cheap shots, interceptions\n212\t    17-setpieces.js   ruck, scrum, lineout, kick-off, 22 drop-out, set kicks, penalty choice\n213\t    18-ai.js          team shape, carrier decisions, defence, set-piece AI, difficulty, referee AI\n214\t    19-arcade.js      hype, supers, ON FIRE, power-ups, venue hazards, ref down\n215\t    20-intent.js      Intent type, device → intent, mouse picking, auto-switch, controller seats\n216\t    30-render.js      pitch and stadium drawing, actors, ball, crowd, lights, weather, FX\n217\t    31-hud.js         score bug, meters, player cards, prompts and glyph badges, radar, banners, markers\n218\t    32-voice.js       commentary director, babble synth, referee calls\n219\t    33-audio.js       songs (layers), SFX definitions, crowd bed\n220\t    34-replay.js      state ring buffer, replay playback\n221\t    40-ui.js          BigMenu helpers, cards, letterbox, 10-foot widgets, CSS for the HTML shell\n222\t    41-scenes-front.js  title, main menu, attract wiring, touch-only screen, about, records\n223\t    42-scenes-modes.js  cup hub, story scene, join, exhibition setup, training selector, results, credits\n224\t    43-scene-match.js   the match scene (live, drill, attract, session variants)\n225\t    50-dev.js         dev panel, tuning sliders, cheats, events, debug overlays, hotkeys\n226\t    60-net.js         AI Hub session: connect, card, host, client, snapshots, intents, reporting\n227\t    90-start.js       new E.Game, settings, boot steps, game.start, game.debug, window.__thugby\n228\t  page.html          the page template (head: session client, hub metas; body: boot splash, canvas)\n229\t  build.mjs          builds build/game.js and the final single HTML in the parent folder\n230\t  test/run.mjs       the one test command (§19)\n231\t  test/lib/*.mjs     vm loader, fake AIHub network, helpers\n232\t  test/*.test.mjs    suites\n233\t  test/browser.mjs   Playwright checks (opt-in flag)\n234\t  test/hub.mjs       AI Hub harness runs (opt-in flag)\n235\t  tools/             copies of the handoff tools (check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-*.mjs) + package.json\n236\t  hub/               harness.html, bridge.js, round.js from ai-hub lib/hub (local testing only)\n237\t  docs/DATA.md       data formats for designers (teams, venues, power-ups, drills, story, commentary, TUNE)\n238\t```\n239\t\n240\t### 4.3 Core objects\n241\t\n242\t- **`TT`**: one namespace object inside the IIFE holding every module's exports (`TT.Match`, `TT.TUNE`, ...).\n243\t  `window.__thugby = { game, TT }` for tests and tools.\n244\t- **`TUNE`**: the balance registry. Each entry `{ key, label, group, value, min, max, step, unit }` (table in §14.2).\n245\t  Simulation code reads `TUNE.v.runSpeed` (the live value), never a literal. `TUNE.reset(key?)`, `TUNE.set(key, v)`\n246\t  (clamped), `TUNE.defaults()`. Difficulty and cheats are layers over it (`TUNE.layer('difficulty', {...})`), so\n247\t  \"reset to defaults\" restores the base values and keeps the chosen difficulty.\n248\t- **RNG**: `TT.rng = E.rng(seed)` per match (one stream for the simulation; a second, `TT.fxRng`, for cosmetic\n249\t  choices). Simulation code never calls `Math.random`. Seeds: a fresh one per match standalone; fixed in tests; from\n250\t  `AIHub.rng('match')` online.\n251\t- **Event bus**: `TT.bus.on(name, fn)`, `TT.bus.emit(name, data)`. The simulation emits; commentary, audio, hype, stats,\n252\t  replays, banners, rumble, records and the attract director listen. Events (with data): `kickoff, pass, catch,\n253\t  dummy, offload, knockOn, forwardPass, interception, tackle, brokenTackle, bigHit, monsterHit, missedTackle,\n254\t  diveTackle, gangHold, fend, sidestep, spin, lineBreak, ruck, ruckWon, jackal, useIt, turnover, scrum, scrumEngage,\n255\t  scrumWon, pushoverTry, lineout, lineoutWon, notStraight, kick, kickCaught, touch, deadBall, dropOut, try,\n256\t  conversion, penaltyGoal, dropGoal, foul, advantage, advantageOver, penalty, choice, tapGo, freeKick, card, sinBinIn,\n257\t  sinBinOut, penaltyTry, superStart, superEnd, onFire, fireOut, powerupThrow, powerupTake, powerupEnd, hazardWarn,\n258\t  hazard, refDown, refUp, hooter, halftime, fulltime, suddenDeath, replayStart, replayEnd`.\n259\t- **`MatchState`** (plain object, serialisable; the whole truth of a match): `{ t, seed, phase, sub, phaseT, half,\n260\t  clock, hooter, suddenDeath, score:[h,a], hype:[h,a], dir:[+1,-1], possession, lastTouch, advantage, ball, players[14],\n261\t  ref, powerups[], hazards[], setpiece, cards[], stats, events[] }`. A player record: `{ id, team, role, x, y, z, vx,\n262\t  vy, facing, st, stT, turbo, stun, flags, ctl, anim }` where `anim` is the compact rig-drive record (§11.1).\n263\t- **`Intent`** (what one on-field player wants this step): `{ move:[x,y] (ground direction, length ≤ 1), turbo,\n264\t  press:{ x, sq, ci, tri, l1, r1 }, held:{ x, sq, ci, l1, r1 }, released:{ x, ci, l1, r1 }, aim:[x,y]|null (a ground\n265\t  point: mouse or stick target), click:{ id }|null (a teammate clicked), t (time stamp for timing mini-games) }`.\n266\t  Buttons are named by the pad's face (`x` = ✕ / A, `sq` = □ / X, `ci` = ○ / B, `tri` = △ / Y) because their meaning\n267\t  depends on context (§6.1).\n268\t- **Controller seats**: `TT.seats = [{ kind: 'local' | 'remote' | 'script', input, team, playerId, color, name }]`.\n269\t  Each step `TT.control.assign()` (auto-switch, §7.2) decides which on-field player each seat drives; every other player\n270\t  gets an AI intent.\n271\t\n272\t### 4.4 Fixed step, determinism and speed\n273\t\n274\t- The engine runs `update(dt)` at exactly 120 steps per second. The match simulation steps once per update with that\n275\t  `dt` (times `TUNE.v.gameSpeed`). Nothing in the simulation reads wall-clock time.\n276\t- Same seed + same intents = same match (the kit-style proof, §19.3, hashes it). Timers are counted in seconds of\n277\t  match time, not frames.\n278\t- Presentation work (rig updates, particles, camera) happens in the scene's update after the simulation step, guarded by\n279\t  `TT.cfg.present` (true in the browser, false by default headless) so a full headless match runs in about a second.\n280\t  Tests that check animation state turn it on.\n281\t\n282\t### 4.5 `game.debug`\n283\t\n284\t```js\n285\tgame.debug = {\n286\t  snapshot() { return { scene, phase, sub, half, clock, score, hype, possession, ball: {x,y,z,state,holder},\n287\t    players: [...{ id, team, role, x, y, z, st, ctl }], ref: { x, y, facing, down }, setpiece, cards, powerups,\n288\t    hazards, lastEvents, cup: { round, results }, attract, dev: { open }, session, replay } },\n289\t  teleport(id, x, y), give(id), force(name, opts),   // force: every dev EVENT (§14.4); the attract director uses it too\n290\t  tune(key, value), seed(n), step(seconds), drill(id), go(scene, data)\n291\t};\n292\t```\n293\t\n294\t### 4.6 Build and bundle\n295\t\n296\t`node build.mjs`:\n297\t1. Concatenates `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })();` into\n298\t   `build/game.js` (with `/* ==== file ==== */` separators).\n299\t2. Writes the final HTML to `../ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828.html` from `page.html`:\n300\t   `<!doctype html>`, `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then **the session client inline as the first script in `<head>`**\n301\t   (unmodified, its `/*! aihub-session 1.4.0` banner intact), then the viewport meta, the hub tags (§15.2), the\n302\t   catalogue tags (`hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4), `<title>Thugby Turbo</title>`, styles (full-screen canvas, the\n303\t   10-foot CSS for the HTML shell, §10.6), then in `<body>`: `E.bootSplash('THUGBY TURBO')` markup, the canvas, the\n304\t   engine inline, the game inline.\n305\t3. Escapes any `</script` inside inlined code (`<\\/script`).\n306\t4. Prints the output size.\n307\t\n308\t`page.html` is also the dev page: open it with `?dev=src` to load the files separately for debugging. Never edit the\n309\tfinal HTML by hand.\n310\t\n311\t---\n312\t\n313\t## 5. The pitch, the stadium and the camera\n314\t\n315\t### 5.1 Geometry (world units; 16 = one tile; display metres = units / 10.24, so the field is \"100 m\")\n316\t\n317\t| Feature | Value |\n318\t|---|---|\n319\t| Field of play (try line to try line) | 1024 (64 tiles): `X0` to `X1`, halfway `CX` |\n320\t| In-goal depth | 96 each end; dead-ball lines at `X0 - 96`, `X1 + 96` |\n321\t| Width (touchline to touchline) | 576 (36 tiles): `Y0` to `Y1`, centre `CY` |\n322\t| Apron (grass beyond touch and dead-ball lines) | 48 |\n323\t| 22 m lines | `X0 + 225`, `X1 - 225` |\n324\t| 10 m lines (dashed) | `CX ± 102` |\n325\t| 5 m lines (dashed, parallel to the try lines) | `X0 + 51`, `X1 - 51` |\n326\t| 5 m and 15 m lines (dashed, parallel to touch) | `Y0 + 51`, `Y0 + 154`, `Y1 - 154`, `Y1 - 51` |\n327\t| Goalposts | on each try line at `CY ± 29`; crossbar z 34; uprights to z 120; padding z 0-20 in the home colours |\n328\t| Corner flags | the four corners of the field and of each in-goal |\n329\t| Sin bin | a green dumpster stencilled SIN BIN, south of the halfway line on the apron (`CX + 60, Y1 + 30`) |\n330\t\n331\tThe TileMap is about 90 x 50 cells (no chunking needed): the field and in-goals are floor (`'grass'`, `'ingoal'`,\n332\t`'apron'`, `'mud'` tags); the **north side** (far side, top of the screen) has a stand of three tiers (`h` 34, 44, 54,\n333\t`face: 'stone'` or `'plank'` by venue) with the crowd on it; **both ends** have two-tier stands behind the dead-ball\n334\tlines; the **south side** (near side, bottom of the screen) has only advertising boards (`h` 14, `cut: true, cutH: 4`,\n335\tso they drop low when they would hide play) and the dugouts. Bodies are stopped by stand walls; the ball is not stopped\n336\tby anything except the ground (out of play is a rule, not a wall).\n337\t\n338\t### 5.2 Pitch drawing\n339\t\n340\t`floorTex(x, y, tag)` like the sports kit's: mown stripes 32 wide alternating along x; grain from `E.hash2`; worn\n341\tlines (white, 2 px; dashed lines 16 on, 16 off); in-goal areas a slightly different green with the home crest painted\n342\tlarge (a 96x96 stencil from a string sprite, at 60% strength); `mud` patches brown with puddle highlights (venue 1);\n343\tscorch marks are decals (lightning, flame jets). Venue colours tint the grass (`E.tones`).\n344\t\n345\t### 5.3 Camera\n346\t\n347\t- Default view `'broadcast'` (yaw 0, pitch 40). `views: ['broadcast', 'threequarter', 'topdown']`; R3, V or Settings,\n348\t  Camera cycles them. Camera zoom: the scene's `camera: { zoom: [.7, 1.4], start: .9 }`.\n349\t- Focus each update: the ball (or its carrier) plus 40 units of lead in the attacking direction, y pulled 25% toward\n350\t  `CY` so both sides of the line show; `cam.smooth = .22`; `cam.bounds` to the stadium.\n351\t- A kick in the air longer than 60 units: `cam.frame([ball, landing], { margin: 40, zoom: [.7, 1] })` until caught or\n352\t  bounced; then back to follow.\n353\t- Set pieces: focus on the mark at zoom 1.15 (scrum, lineout), 1.0 (ruck: stays on follow).\n354\t- Replays: zoom 1.35 on the key actor, letterbox bars, 0.5x.\n355\t- Controlled players and the ball carrier draw with `xray: { actors: true }` so they stay findable in a crowd.\n356\t\n357\t### 5.4 Behind-the-kicker camera (conversions and penalty goals)\n358\t\n359\tA second custom view: `E.VIEWS.posts = new E.View('posts', 'Kicker', yaw, 22, 1, 1)` with yaw chosen so the target\n360\tposts are straight up the screen (90 or 270 depending on which end: **verify the sign in the M3 spike** by rendering\n361\tthe posts and checking the crossbar is level and far). The TileMap re-bakes per view the first time (one boot step:\n362\t\"Painting the posts\"). Fallback if it looks wrong: stay in `'broadcast'` and show an inset aim gauge.\n363\t\n364\t---\n365\t\n366\t## 6. Controls (10-foot, controller-first)\n367\t\n368\t### 6.1 Gamepad (any pad in the browser's standard mapping; PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox in brackets)\n369\t\n370\t| Button | With the ball | Without the ball | In menus |\n371\t|---|---|---|---|\n372\t| Left stick / D-pad | run; aims kicks | run | move the focus |\n373\t| R2 [RT] (held) | TURBO | TURBO | |\n374\t| L1 [LB] | pass up the screen (tap: short; hold: long, skips a man); in a tackle: offload | switch to the teammate above you on the line | previous tab |\n375\t| R1 [RB] | pass down the screen (as L1); in a tackle: offload | switch to the teammate below you | next tab |\n376\t| ✕ [A] | sidestep (stick side; tap twice: spin); in the in-goal: dive to ground it | tackle (tap); hold and let go in range: BIG HIT | confirm |\n377\t| □ [X] | fend (hand-off) | dive tackle | reset a slider (dev) |\n378\t| ○ [B] | kick: tap = grubber; hold = punt (power meter, stick aims, stick pulled back at release = bomb); in the opponents' 22, hold = drop goal | switch to the player nearest the ball | back |\n379\t| △ [Y] | SUPER: Thunder Run (Hype full) | SUPER: Hurricane Hit (Hype full) | context action (hub card: more time) |\n380\t| Options [Menu] | pause | pause | |\n381\t| R3 | camera view | camera view | |\n382\t| Set pieces | ✕ times and mashes; L1/R1 alternate to push (scrum) and pick a pod (lineout); ○ powers set kicks | | |\n383\t\n384\tPass **\"up\"** means toward the far touchline (top of the screen), **\"down\"** toward the near one, whichever way the team\n385\tattacks. Floating glyph tags over the two receivers say which button reaches whom (§10.5), so nobody has to think about\n386\tit. Options is ignored while Create is held (the AI Hub's corner bar).\n387\t\n388\t### 6.2 Keyboard and mouse (the same game; PC keys)\n389\t\n390\t| Key / mouse | With the ball | Without the ball | In menus |\n391\t|---|---|---|---|\n392\t| W A S D (or arrows) | run | run | move the focus |\n393\t| Shift (held) | turbo | turbo | |\n394\t| Q / E | pass up / down (hold: long); offload | switch up / down the line | tabs |\n395\t| Space | sidestep (double-tap: spin); dive in the in-goal | tackle; hold → BIG HIT | confirm (Enter too) |\n396\t| F | fend | dive tackle | |\n397\t| C | kick (tap / hold; drop goal in their 22) | switch to the player nearest the ball | |\n398\t| R | super | super | |\n399\t| Esc / P | pause | pause | back |\n400\t| Mouse move | hovering a teammate highlights him and shows whether a pass to him is legal (green) or forward (red) | hovering a teammate highlights him | hover moves the focus |\n401\t| Left click | pass to the hovered teammate | take control of the hovered teammate | pick |\n402\t| Right button (hold, let go) | kick toward the cursor: a landing ring follows the mouse, the meter fills toward it | | |\n403\t| Wheel / - = | camera zoom (engine) | | scroll |\n404\t| V | camera view | | |\n405\t| ` (backquote) | dev panel, once Dev Mode is on (§14) | | |\n406\t| Set pieces | Space times and mashes; Q/E alternate (scrum) or pick a pod (also W/S, or click a pod); set kicks: the mouse aims, hold the left button for power | | |\n407\t\n408\tThe mouse is native, not a stand-in: clicking a teammate passes or switches, the cursor aims kicks, hover focuses every\n409\tmenu and card, clicking a lineout pod picks it, and set kicks aim and power with the mouse.\n410\t\n411\t### 6.3 Presets and two players on one keyboard\n412\t\n413\tOne preset object `KEYS`, built as the sports kit builds its own: start from `E.Input.DEFAULT` (its moves and its menu\n414\tactions `start pause confirm cancel`), delete `attack dash skill jump`, add `x, sq, ci, tri, l1, r1, turbo` with the\n415\tcodes in the tables above (pad codes: `Pad0` ✕, `Pad1` ○, `Pad2` □, `Pad3` △, `Pad4` L1, `Pad5` R1, `Pad7` R2,\n416\t`Pad9` Options, `Pad11` R3; L2 is left free). Use it on every play scene (`input: KEYS`). `KEYS.halves` (patch 2):\n417\t\n418\t| Half | Move | Turbo | ✕ | □ | ○ | △ | L1 | R1 | Start / pause |\n419\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n420\t| left (P1) | WASD | LeftShift | Space | F | C | R | Q | E | 1 / Esc |\n421\t| right (P2) | arrows | Comma, Numpad0 | Period, Numpad1 | Slash, Numpad2 | Semicolon, Numpad3 | Quote, Numpad6 | KeyK, Numpad4 | KeyL, Numpad5 | 2, Enter / Backspace |\n422\t\n423\t`menu: { controls: { x: 'Sidestep / Tackle', sq: 'Fend / Dive tackle', ci: 'Kick / Switch to ball', tri: 'Super',\n424\tl1: 'Pass up / Switch up', r1: 'Pass down / Switch down', turbo: 'Turbo' } }` names them on the Controls screen; every\n425\tone is rebindable there.\n426\t\n427\t### 6.4 Prompts and glyphs follow the last device\n428\t\n429\t- Every prompt is built from actions, never literal keys: `TT.glyph(g, action, x, y, input)` draws a badge for the\n430\t  device in `input.lastDevice` (`'gamepad'` → a pad badge in the chosen family; `'keyboard'` → a keycap with\n431\t  `input.codeLabel(code)`; `'mouse'` → a mouse icon with the button lit when the action has a mouse binding, else the\n432\t  keycap). Text prompts use markup `\"{b:x} TACKLE  {b:ci} SWITCH\"` parsed by `TT.prompt()`.\n433\t- Override the standard `glyphs` setting from the game: `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Display', label: 'Controller\n434\t  buttons', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △'], ['xbox', 'Xbox A B X\n435\t  Y']], apply: (g, v) => g.players.forEach(p => p.glyphs = v) }`, and set `input.glyphs` on each joiner. It then shows\n436\t  in Settings, Display (the settings screen), where TASK.md wants it.\n437\t- Badges: PlayStation ✕ blue `#6a9cf0`, ○ red `#f05a64`, □ pink `#e07ac8`, △ green `#4ad0a0` on a dark disc; L1/R1/R2\n438\t  as rounded pills. Xbox A green `#5ac85a`, B red `#e8503a`, X blue `#3a86e8`, Y yellow `#f0c830`; LB/RB/RT pills.\n439\t  Keycaps light grey with a dark shadow, wide for Space and Shift. All string sprites designed at 9-11 px, drawn at\n440\t  scale 1 in the HUD (scale 2 on big prompts).\n441\t\n442\t### 6.5 Assists\n443\t\n444\tSettings, Game: **Auto-switch** (Assisted: to the defender nearest the ball each time the ball is passed or kicked, and\n445\ton ✕ when no one is in range; Full: always the best defender; Manual: only by buttons and mouse). **Pass assist** (On:\n446\ta pass button with no legal receiver on that side throws to the nearest legal one on the other side; Off: it is a\n447\tdummy, §7.4). **Hold or toggle** turbo.\n448\t\n449\t### 6.6 Touch-only devices\n450\t\n451\t- Detect: `matchMedia('(any-pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches` and no gamepad\n452\t  has been seen and no key pressed. Re-check on `gamepadconnected`, `keydown` and `pointermove` with a mouse: any of\n453\t  them leaves touch-only mode at once.\n454\t- In touch-only mode: no touch buttons or stick anywhere (`game.input.touchEnabled = false`, every scene `touch: []`);\n455\t  the title shows **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED** (scale 2) with \"Connect a controller or a keyboard to play.\" and a\n456\t  big **SETTINGS** button (tap → `game.menu('settings')`); the attract mode still starts after 10 s; a tap during it\n457\t  goes back to the title.\n458\t\n459\t---\n460\t\n461\t## 7. Match systems (rules and numbers)\n462\t\n463\tAll numbers are `TUNE` defaults (§14.2). \"u\" = world units.\n464\t\n465\t### 7.1 Match flow and clock\n466\t\n467\tPhases (`state.phase`), each with a watchdog (any phase longer than its limit resolves itself and emits a `watchdog`\n468\tevent that tests fail on):\n469\t\n470\t```"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"aa61a3bf-b325-41ab-acc5-c98b5e68fe02","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:56:30.992Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-065619__xa7e588/work/PLAN.md","content":"# THUGBY TURBO\n\n*Over-the-top arcade rugby sevens. Big hits, bigger hair, seven a side.*\n\nThis is the build plan for the developers (people or AI models) who will make the demo described in `TASK.md`. Read\n`TASK.md` first, then this file. Everything the build needs is here or in the engine's handoff package. Every engine API\nnamed below was checked against **my-3D2dge v0.85.2** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/tree/main/dist/handoff`) and\nthe **AI Hub Session Protocol v1, client 1.4.0** (`github.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/tree/main/lib/hub`). Where this plan\nsays \"verify\", the builder checks the detail in a spike before relying on it.\n\n---\n\n## 0. Read this first\n\n**The core loop, in one sentence (put it at the top of the game's source):**\n*Run at their line, pass it backwards, smash through or step round them, ground it over the line and kick the\nconversion, while seven lunatics in the other colours try to flatten you, and the referee looks the other way.*\n\n**Where things are:** the game in one page (§2); architecture (§4); controls (§6); every rule and number (§7); content\n(§9); build order (§17); risks (§18); tests (§19); the done list (§21).\n\n**Working rules on this machine (from TASK.md):** keep every dev file in the dev subfolder; read no files outside what\nthe build needs (other agents share the directory, ports and processes); prefer `file://` and the engine's headless\ntools; a server (only the AI Hub harness needs one) binds `127.0.0.1` on a port the OS picks, and you confirm the page\nis yours before trusting a result; stop only processes you started, by PID (never `pkill`, `killall`, `fuser -k`).\n\n**Hard requirements from TASK.md, and where this plan covers each:**\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Where |\n|---|---|\n| Title riffing on the source, picked by a real RNG | §1 |\n| Genre and perspective that fit the source | §2, §5 |\n| A single HTML file, a dev subfolder, the file names | §1, §4.6 |\n| Built on the engine's handoff package; changes allowed | §3 |\n| Native art style plus new rugby art; native animation plus new rugby animations | §10, §11 |\n| All audio written in code; Animalese-style gibberish voices | §12 |\n| AI Hub multiplayer, and multiplayer on its own | §15 |\n| A feature-complete, polished vertical slice that shows every mechanic | §7, §8, §9 |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | §14 |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | §13 |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; native keyboard and mouse; glyphs follow the last device; Xbox option | §6, §10.6 |\n| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\" plus Settings; attract still runs | §6.6 |\n| Writing in the source's style | §9.3, §9.5 |\n| No TODOs, placeholders or stubs | §21 |\n| Source info and feedback in the chat reply and an in-game About/Credits screen; exit-interview.json | §20 |\n| Headless tests behind one command; a scripted playthrough of the whole game | §19 |\n\n---\n\n## 1. Title, names and deliverables\n\n**Title: THUGBY TURBO.** Picked as TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on the word \"rugby\" were brainstormed silently,\nthen one was drawn with a real random number generator: Python's `secrets.randbelow(50)` (OS entropy) returned 16,\nentry #17 of 50: **\"Thugby Turbo\"**. Record this in the About screen. Do not re-pick it.\n\n| Thing | Name |\n|---|---|\n| Title on screen | THUGBY TURBO |\n| GameTitle in file names | `ThugbyTurbo` |\n| SOURCE in file names | `Rugby` |\n| Dev subfolder (in this folder) | `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828/` |\n| The deliverable (in this folder, beside PLAN.md) | `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828.html` |\n| Exit interview (in this folder, beside the HTML) | `exit-interview.json` |\n| Page `<title>` | `Thugby Turbo` |\n| Settings/save id (`new E.Game({ id })`) | `thugby-turbo` |\n\nThe HTML is one self-contained file: the AI Hub session client first in `<head>`, then the engine and the game inlined.\nNo CDN is needed (the engine has no dependencies); do not add any.\n\n---\n\n## 2. The game in one page\n\n**Genre:** arcade sports, rugby sevens played for laughs and for keeps (NBA Jam's turbo and \"on fire\", Mutant League's\ncartoon violence, Speedball's crowd-thrown power-ups), wrapped in a short story campaign, with couch and online party\nmultiplayer for 1 to 4 players. Sevens is the real format that suits an arcade game best: seven a side on a full\npitch, short halves, open space, sudden-death extra time, and the scoring team kicks off.\n\n**Perspective:** a TV broadcast camera. The pitch runs left to right across the screen, seen from the stand at a 40°\npitch (a custom engine view, `'broadcast'`), panning with the ball. Heights read well (kicks, lineout jumps, the\nposts), and passing across the pitch is up and down the screen. Players can switch to a high 55° camera\n(`'threequarter'`) or near-overhead (`'topdown'`). Conversions and penalty kicks cut to a behind-the-kicker camera\n(§5.4).\n\n**Tone:** a live Saturday broadcast on CHANNEL THUD. Two commentators (breathless Barry Bellows and bone-dry Ros\nRucker), a whistle-happy, short-sighted referee, a gruff coach with rules for everything, rival captains who trash-talk\nin gibberish with subtitles, mud, meat pies, a giant chicken mascot and a sin bin that is an actual bin. Affectionate,\nloud, never mean.\n\n**What makes it rugby (all in the demo):** passing only backwards (L1 and R1 pass up and down the line); tackles that\nend in rucks; tries you must ground, then a conversion from in line with where you scored; scrums, lineouts, kick-offs,\n22 drop-outs, penalties with a choice (tap, kick at goal, kick to touch), drop goals, knock-ons and forward passes,\noffside lines, advantage, yellow cards and the sin bin, the hooter that waits for the ball to go dead.\n\n**What makes it over the top (all in the demo):** a turbo button; big hits charged by holding the button that launch\npeople into orbit; sidesteps, spins, fends and dummies; a team HYPE meter that unlocks supers (THUNDER RUN, HURRICANE\nHIT); a player who scores twice running is ON FIRE; fans throw power-ups onto the pitch (meat pie, iron shoulders, mega\nboot, grease jersey, air horn); each stadium has a hazard (mud, a chicken pitch invader, flame jets, lightning); the\nreferee only calls foul play he can see, and he can be flattened (\"REF DOWN, ANYTHING GOES!\"); instant replays of the\nbiggest moments; a boss who needs two tacklers.\n\n**Modes:** MEGA CUP (the story: training plus four matches, about 25-35 minutes), EXHIBITION (any two teams, any\nstadium, 1-4 local players, versus or co-op), TRAINING GROUND (nine drills, each also a tutorial), RECORDS (arcade\ntables with initials), SETTINGS, ABOUT & CREDITS. Online: AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2-4 players (§15.2).\n\n---\n\n## 3. Tech stack and the engine\n\n**Base:** the my-3D2dge handoff package v0.85.2, full engine (`my-3d2dge.js`, 1.2 MB, readable, every module inside).\nIt is a 2D engine with a 3D world: x east, y south, z up, 16 world units a tile; characters are procedural 3D skeletons\n(`E.Humanoid`) drawn as pixel art; `res: 'ps1'` (320x240) with `expand: true` fills any screen (about 427x240 on 16:9).\nCanvas 2D and Web Audio only. No other libraries.\n\n**Research done for this plan** (the builder may cite it under Research, adding its own): the handoff package's\n`README.md`, `API.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `kits/README.md`, `kits/sports.js`, parts of `kits/brawler.js`, the template\n`game/`, `tools/check.mjs`, the headers of `modules/story.js` and `modules/arcade.js`, and the engine source around\nsettings, the shell, input labels, half keyboards, dialogs and actions; the AI Hub `lib/hub/README.md`, `retrofit.md`,\n`session.js` and `examples/arena.html` (the shared-world pattern).\n\n**Read before writing code (in this order):** `API.md` whole; `kits/sports.js` whole (the closest kit: a ball as an\n`E.Body`, passes led to the receiver, `E.arc` lobs, formations, a match clock, `allowJoin`, a radio commentator, a\ndeterministic `run()` proof and a `bot()`); `kits/brawler.js` around its knockdown code (`knock`, `getUp`, launch arcs);\n`AI_GUIDE.md` sections Standard features, Frame order, Players, Humanoid, Audio, Shell, Testing without a browser,\nRecorded sessions, Sports recipe, Building a big game; `docs/ASSETS.md` before drawing any prop; `modules/story.js` and\n`modules/arcade.js` headers. Search `API-INDEX.md` for names; do not read it whole.\n\n**Engine features this game uses:** scenes and `game.go` transitions; `TileMap` (the pitch, stands, boards) with\n`floorTex`, `cut` walls, floor tags; `E.Body`; `E.Humanoid` and `rig.drawPortrait`; `E.ACTIONS` / `E.Action` (paired\nanimations: the tackle is a takedown's cousin); `E.MOVES` and custom attack specs; `E.arc`; `E.sees` and `r.sightCone`\n(the referee's eyes); `r.marker` (off-screen arrows); `r.pick` and `game.mouseGround` (mouse in the world);\nparticles, `game.hitFx`, `freeze`, `shake`, `flash`; canvas lighting; `game.weather`; `game.tween`, `game.script`,\n`E.until`; `E.Dialog` (commentary radio, `modal: false`), `E.Story` (cutscenes as data), `E.Menu`; `E.Arcade` (attract\ncycle, records with initials, CONTINUE countdown); `game.settings` (own settings), the standard pause, settings and\ncontrols screens (`menu: { items, settings, controls, quit }`); `game.saves`; `game.allowJoin` / `addPlayer` (local\nmultiplayer); `game.audio` music (named layers), `sting`, `define`, `sfx`; `game.debug`, `game.advance`,\n`input.tap/hold/press/feed` (tests); `E.rng`.\n\n**Facts found during research that the build must respect:**\n- The pixel font has no `✕ ○ □ △` glyphs: `input.label('x')` returns `'✕'` for a PlayStation pad, which the canvas\n  font draws as `?`. Fix in the engine font (patch 1) and draw coloured button badges for prompts anyway (§10.6).\n- The standard setting `glyphs` (Controller group) defaults to `'auto'`. TASK.md wants PlayStation by default: override\n  it from the game (§6.4). Its `apply` only touches `game.input`; apply it to every player and to joiners.\n- `Input.halfKeys` gives two keyboard players fixed button lists (F G H R T Y V B / . / , L ; ' K M) in the order of the\n  preset's actions. Our layouts (§6.3) need patch 2.\n- `E.Menu` has no font scale; 10-foot menus need patch 3 (or a small BigMenu of our own).\n- `E.Dialog` blips one sound every second letter; Animalese needs a per-letter hook (patch 4).\n- While `game.paused`, or during hit-stop (`game.freeze`), the scene's `update` does not run (only input ticks); at\n  `game.timeScale = 0` it runs with `dt` 0 (so a dev panel can still read input). In an AI Hub shared world nothing may\n  stop the host's world: sessions use `pausable: false`, `autoPause: false`, `corner: false`, `menu: { quit: false }`\n  and no `game.freeze` (§15.2).\n- `E.VIEWS` takes custom views: `E.VIEWS.broadcast = new E.View('broadcast', 'Broadcast', 0, 40, 1, 1)`, then scenes\n  name `'broadcast'`. `E.charView` draws rigs from the sprite-like angle at pitch 40 to 89.\n- Headless (Node, no DOM) `new E.Game()` skips drawing; `rig.update` still costs time. Keep the simulation free of rig\n  state so tests can skip rig updates (§4.4).\n- The settings screen lists every settings group except Controls, Controller and Touch, so dev sliders must not be\n  `game.settings` items (they would show in Settings); the dev panel is the game's own (§14).\n\n**Engine patches (allowed by TASK.md; keep each small, list them in `engine/PATCHES.md` and the About screen's Engine\nFeedback):**\n\n| # | Patch | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | Add `✕ ○ □ △` to the 5x7 font (`G57`) | PlayStation labels render as `?` |\n| 2 | `Input.halfKeys(preset, side)` uses `preset.halves[side]` (per-action code lists) when present | two keyboard players need sensible layouts |\n| 3 | `E.Menu` option `scale` (font scale, row height, box padding) | 10-foot menus |\n| 4 | `E.Dialog` option/field `onLetter(ch, index, text)` called for each letter typed; `voice: null` keeps the default blip off | per-letter babble voices |\n\nAnything else found missing during the build (a bug, a gap) may be patched the same way and listed.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Architecture\n\n### 4.1 Layers and the one rule that makes everything else work\n\n```\n Devices (pad, keys, mouse)   AI brains   Network intents   Test scripts   Attract director\n              \\                   |              |                |              /\n               '----------->  INTENTS (one per on-field player, per step)  <----'\n                                          |\n                                   MATCH SIMULATION  (rules, physics, contact, set pieces, AI-free)\n                                          |\n                                 MATCH STATE (plain, serialisable)  ---> replay ring buffer\n                                          |                         ---> network snapshots (host)\n                                   PRESENTATION  (rigs, camera, HUD, FX, audio, commentary)\n```\n\n**The rule: the simulation reads only intents and writes only `MatchState`; presentation reads only `MatchState` and\nevents.** Then the same presentation draws a live match, an instant replay (recorded states), an online client\n(interpolated snapshots) and the attract demo; the same simulation runs headless for tests, as the host of an online\nmatch, and for the AI Hub \"server\" seat with nothing drawn.\n\n### 4.2 Source layout (the dev subfolder)\n\n```\nThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828/\n  engine/my-3d2dge.js        handoff engine v0.85.2 + patches (engine/PATCHES.md lists them)\n  vendor/aihub-session.js    lib/hub/session.js 1.4.0, byte-for-byte unmodified\n  src/                       the game, concatenated in file-name order into one IIFE by build.mjs\n    00-core.js        TT namespace, constants, TUNE registry, RNG, event bus, helpers, config flags\n    01-data-teams.js  teams, kits, rosters, AI styles\n    02-data-venues.js stadiums, weather, hazards, crowd, boards, skylines\n    03-data-content.js power-ups, commentary lines, story nodes, drills, records, credits text\n    10-pitch.js       pitch geometry, TileMap, line texture, posts, stands, boards, sin bin\n    11-ball.js        ball states, flight, oval bounce, out of play\n    12-players.js     player entities, stats, movement, turbo, states, control assignment\n    13-anim.js        custom E.ACTIONS, attack specs, rig post-processing (carry, dive...), anim-state codes\n    14-laws.js        forward pass, knock-on, offside lines, touch, scoring, fouls, advantage, cards\n    15-match.js       match state machine: phases, clock, halves, hooter, restarts, sudden death, stats\n    16-contact.js     tackles, big hits, dive and gang tackles, fends, sidesteps, cheap shots, interceptions\n    17-setpieces.js   ruck, scrum, lineout, kick-off, 22 drop-out, set kicks, penalty choice\n    18-ai.js          team shape, carrier decisions, defence, set-piece AI, difficulty, referee AI\n    19-arcade.js      hype, supers, ON FIRE, power-ups, venue hazards, ref down\n    20-intent.js      Intent type, device → intent, mouse picking, auto-switch, controller seats\n    30-render.js      pitch and stadium drawing, actors, ball, crowd, lights, weather, FX\n    31-hud.js         score bug, meters, player cards, prompts and glyph badges, radar, banners, markers\n    32-voice.js       commentary director, babble synth, referee calls\n    33-audio.js       songs (layers), SFX definitions, crowd bed\n    34-replay.js      state ring buffer, replay playback\n    40-ui.js          BigMenu helpers, cards, letterbox, 10-foot widgets, CSS for the HTML shell\n    41-scenes-front.js  title, main menu, attract wiring, touch-only screen, about, records\n    42-scenes-modes.js  cup hub, story scene, join, exhibition setup, training selector, results, credits\n    43-scene-match.js   the match scene (live, drill, attract, session variants)\n    50-dev.js         dev panel, tuning sliders, cheats, events, debug overlays, hotkeys\n    60-net.js         AI Hub session: connect, card, host, client, snapshots, intents, reporting\n    90-start.js       new E.Game, settings, boot steps, game.start, game.debug, window.__thugby\n  page.html          the page template (head: session client, hub metas; body: boot splash, canvas)\n  build.mjs          builds build/game.js and the final single HTML in the parent folder\n  test/run.mjs       the one test command (§19)\n  test/lib/*.mjs     vm loader, fake AIHub network, helpers\n  test/*.test.mjs    suites\n  test/browser.mjs   Playwright checks (opt-in flag)\n  test/hub.mjs       AI Hub harness runs (opt-in flag)\n  tools/             copies of the handoff tools (check.mjs, filmstrip.mjs, replay.mjs, asset-*.mjs) + package.json\n  hub/               harness.html, bridge.js, round.js from ai-hub lib/hub (local testing only)\n  docs/DATA.md       data formats for designers (teams, venues, power-ups, drills, story, commentary, TUNE)\n```\n\n### 4.3 Core objects\n\n- **`TT`**: one namespace object inside the IIFE holding every module's exports (`TT.Match`, `TT.TUNE`, ...).\n  `window.__thugby = { game, TT }` for tests and tools.\n- **`TUNE`**: the balance registry. Each entry `{ key, label, group, value, min, max, step, unit }` (table in §14.2).\n  Simulation code reads `TUNE.v.runSpeed` (the live value), never a literal. `TUNE.reset(key?)`, `TUNE.set(key, v)`\n  (clamped), `TUNE.defaults()`. Difficulty and cheats are layers over it (`TUNE.layer('difficulty', {...})`), so\n  \"reset to defaults\" restores the base values and keeps the chosen difficulty.\n- **RNG**: `TT.rng = E.rng(seed)` per match (one stream for the simulation; a second, `TT.fxRng`, for cosmetic\n  choices). Simulation code never calls `Math.random`. Seeds: a fresh one per match standalone; fixed in tests; from\n  `AIHub.rng('match')` online.\n- **Event bus**: `TT.bus.on(name, fn)`, `TT.bus.emit(name, data)`. The simulation emits; commentary, audio, hype, stats,\n  replays, banners, rumble, records and the attract director listen. Events (with data): `kickoff, pass, catch,\n  dummy, offload, knockOn, forwardPass, interception, tackle, brokenTackle, bigHit, monsterHit, missedTackle,\n  diveTackle, gangHold, fend, sidestep, spin, lineBreak, ruck, ruckWon, jackal, useIt, turnover, scrum, scrumEngage,\n  scrumWon, pushoverTry, lineout, lineoutWon, notStraight, kick, kickCaught, touch, deadBall, dropOut, try,\n  conversion, penaltyGoal, dropGoal, foul, advantage, advantageOver, penalty, choice, tapGo, freeKick, card, sinBinIn,\n  sinBinOut, penaltyTry, superStart, superEnd, onFire, fireOut, powerupThrow, powerupTake, powerupEnd, hazardWarn,\n  hazard, refDown, refUp, hooter, halftime, fulltime, suddenDeath, replayStart, replayEnd`.\n- **`MatchState`** (plain object, serialisable; the whole truth of a match): `{ t, seed, phase, sub, phaseT, half,\n  clock, hooter, suddenDeath, score:[h,a], hype:[h,a], dir:[+1,-1], possession, lastTouch, advantage, ball, players[14],\n  ref, powerups[], hazards[], setpiece, cards[], stats, events[] }`. A player record: `{ id, team, role, x, y, z, vx,\n  vy, facing, st, stT, turbo, stun, flags, ctl, anim }` where `anim` is the compact rig-drive record (§11.1).\n- **`Intent`** (what one on-field player wants this step): `{ move:[x,y] (ground direction, length ≤ 1), turbo,\n  press:{ x, sq, ci, tri, l1, r1 }, held:{ x, sq, ci, l1, r1 }, released:{ x, ci, l1, r1 }, aim:[x,y]|null (a ground\n  point: mouse or stick target), click:{ id }|null (a teammate clicked), t (time stamp for timing mini-games) }`.\n  Buttons are named by the pad's face (`x` = ✕ / A, `sq` = □ / X, `ci` = ○ / B, `tri` = △ / Y) because their meaning\n  depends on context (§6.1).\n- **Controller seats**: `TT.seats = [{ kind: 'local' | 'remote' | 'script', input, team, playerId, color, name }]`.\n  Each step `TT.control.assign()` (auto-switch, §7.2) decides which on-field player each seat drives; every other player\n  gets an AI intent.\n\n### 4.4 Fixed step, determinism and speed\n\n- The engine runs `update(dt)` at exactly 120 steps per second. The match simulation steps once per update with that\n  `dt` (times `TUNE.v.gameSpeed`). Nothing in the simulation reads wall-clock time.\n- Same seed + same intents = same match (the kit-style proof, §19.3, hashes it). Timers are counted in seconds of\n  match time, not frames.\n- Presentation work (rig updates, particles, camera) happens in the scene's update after the simulation step, guarded by\n  `TT.cfg.present` (true in the browser, false by default headless) so a full headless match runs in about a second.\n  Tests that check animation state turn it on.\n\n### 4.5 `game.debug`\n\n```js\ngame.debug = {\n  snapshot() { return { scene, phase, sub, half, clock, score, hype, possession, ball: {x,y,z,state,holder},\n    players: [...{ id, team, role, x, y, z, st, ctl }], ref: { x, y, facing, down }, setpiece, cards, powerups,\n    hazards, lastEvents, cup: { round, results }, attract, dev: { open }, session, replay } },\n  teleport(id, x, y), give(id), force(name, opts),   // force: every dev EVENT (§14.4); the attract director uses it too\n  tune(key, value), seed(n), step(seconds), drill(id), go(scene, data)\n};\n```\n\n### 4.6 Build and bundle\n\n`node build.mjs`:\n1. Concatenates `src/*.js` in name order inside `(() => { 'use strict'; const E = My3D2dge; ... })();` into\n   `build/game.js` (with `/* ==== file ==== */` separators).\n2. Writes the final HTML to `../ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828.html` from `page.html`:\n   `<!doctype html>`, `<meta charset=\"utf-8\">`, then **the session client inline as the first script in `<head>`**\n   (unmodified, its `/*! aihub-session 1.4.0` banner intact), then the viewport meta, the hub tags (§15.2), the\n   catalogue tags (`hub:type` Game, `hub:players` 1-4), `<title>Thugby Turbo</title>`, styles (full-screen canvas, the\n   10-foot CSS for the HTML shell, §10.6), then in `<body>`: `E.bootSplash('THUGBY TURBO')` markup, the canvas, the\n   engine inline, the game inline.\n3. Escapes any `</script` inside inlined code (`<\\/script`).\n4. Prints the output size.\n\n`page.html` is also the dev page: open it with `?dev=src` to load the files separately for debugging. Never edit the\nfinal HTML by hand.\n\n---\n\n## 5. The pitch, the stadium and the camera\n\n### 5.1 Geometry (world units; 16 = one tile; display metres = units / 10.24, so the field is \"100 m\")\n\n| Feature | Value |\n|---|---|\n| Field of play (try line to try line) | 1024 (64 tiles): `X0` to `X1`, halfway `CX` |\n| In-goal depth | 96 each end; dead-ball lines at `X0 - 96`, `X1 + 96` |\n| Width (touchline to touchline) | 576 (36 tiles): `Y0` to `Y1`, centre `CY` |\n| Apron (grass beyond touch and dead-ball lines) | 48 |\n| 22 m lines | `X0 + 225`, `X1 - 225` |\n| 10 m lines (dashed) | `CX ± 102` |\n| 5 m lines (dashed, parallel to the try lines) | `X0 + 51`, `X1 - 51` |\n| 5 m and 15 m lines (dashed, parallel to touch) | `Y0 + 51`, `Y0 + 154`, `Y1 - 154`, `Y1 - 51` |\n| Goalposts | on each try line at `CY ± 29`; crossbar z 34; uprights to z 120; padding z 0-20 in the home colours |\n| Corner flags | the four corners of the field and of each in-goal |\n| Sin bin | a green dumpster stencilled SIN BIN, south of the halfway line on the apron (`CX + 60, Y1 + 30`) |\n\nThe TileMap is about 90 x 50 cells (no chunking needed): the field and in-goals are floor (`'grass'`, `'ingoal'`,\n`'apron'`, `'mud'` tags); the **north side** (far side, top of the screen) has a stand of three tiers (`h` 34, 44, 54,\n`face: 'stone'` or `'plank'` by venue) with the crowd on it; **both ends** have two-tier stands behind the dead-ball\nlines; the **south side** (near side, bottom of the screen) has only advertising boards (`h` 14, `cut: true, cutH: 4`,\nso they drop low when they would hide play) and the dugouts. Bodies are stopped by stand walls; the ball is not stopped\nby anything except the ground (out of play is a rule, not a wall).\n\n### 5.2 Pitch drawing\n\n`floorTex(x, y, tag)` like the sports kit's: mown stripes 32 wide alternating along x; grain from `E.hash2`; worn\nlines (white, 2 px; dashed lines 16 on, 16 off); in-goal areas a slightly different green with the home crest painted\nlarge (a 96x96 stencil from a string sprite, at 60% strength); `mud` patches brown with puddle highlights (venue 1);\nscorch marks are decals (lightning, flame jets). Venue colours tint the grass (`E.tones`).\n\n### 5.3 Camera\n\n- Default view `'broadcast'` (yaw 0, pitch 40). `views: ['broadcast', 'threequarter', 'topdown']`; R3, V or Settings,\n  Camera cycles them. Camera zoom: the scene's `camera: { zoom: [.7, 1.4], start: .9 }`.\n- Focus each update: the ball (or its carrier) plus 40 units of lead in the attacking direction, y pulled 25% toward\n  `CY` so both sides of the line show; `cam.smooth = .22`; `cam.bounds` to the stadium.\n- A kick in the air longer than 60 units: `cam.frame([ball, landing], { margin: 40, zoom: [.7, 1] })` until caught or\n  bounced; then back to follow.\n- Set pieces: focus on the mark at zoom 1.15 (scrum, lineout), 1.0 (ruck: stays on follow).\n- Replays: zoom 1.35 on the key actor, letterbox bars, 0.5x.\n- Controlled players and the ball carrier draw with `xray: { actors: true }` so they stay findable in a crowd.\n\n### 5.4 Behind-the-kicker camera (conversions and penalty goals)\n\nA second custom view: `E.VIEWS.posts = new E.View('posts', 'Kicker', yaw, 22, 1, 1)` with yaw chosen so the target\nposts are straight up the screen (90 or 270 depending on which end: **verify the sign in the M3 spike** by rendering\nthe posts and checking the crossbar is level and far). The TileMap re-bakes per view the first time (one boot step:\n\"Painting the posts\"). Fallback if it looks wrong: stay in `'broadcast'` and show an inset aim gauge.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Controls (10-foot, controller-first)\n\n### 6.1 Gamepad (any pad in the browser's standard mapping; PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox in brackets)\n\n| Button | With the ball | Without the ball | In menus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Left stick / D-pad | run; aims kicks | run | move the focus |\n| R2 [RT] (held) | TURBO | TURBO | |\n| L1 [LB] | pass up the screen (tap: short; hold: long, skips a man); in a tackle: offload | switch to the teammate above you on the line | previous tab |\n| R1 [RB] | pass down the screen (as L1); in a tackle: offload | switch to the teammate below you | next tab |\n| ✕ [A] | sidestep (stick side; tap twice: spin); in the in-goal: dive to ground it | tackle (tap); hold and let go in range: BIG HIT | confirm |\n| □ [X] | fend (hand-off) | dive tackle | reset a slider (dev) |\n| ○ [B] | kick: tap = grubber; hold = punt (power meter, stick aims, stick pulled back at release = bomb); in the opponents' 22, hold = drop goal | switch to the player nearest the ball | back |\n| △ [Y] | SUPER: Thunder Run (Hype full) | SUPER: Hurricane Hit (Hype full) | context action (hub card: more time) |\n| Options [Menu] | pause | pause | |\n| R3 | camera view | camera view | |\n| Set pieces | ✕ times and mashes; L1/R1 alternate to push (scrum) and pick a pod (lineout); ○ powers set kicks | | |\n\nPass **\"up\"** means toward the far touchline (top of the screen), **\"down\"** toward the near one, whichever way the team\nattacks. Floating glyph tags over the two receivers say which button reaches whom (§10.5), so nobody has to think about\nit. Options is ignored while Create is held (the AI Hub's corner bar).\n\n### 6.2 Keyboard and mouse (the same game; PC keys)\n\n| Key / mouse | With the ball | Without the ball | In menus |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| W A S D (or arrows) | run | run | move the focus |\n| Shift (held) | turbo | turbo | |\n| Q / E | pass up / down (hold: long); offload | switch up / down the line | tabs |\n| Space | sidestep (double-tap: spin); dive in the in-goal | tackle; hold → BIG HIT | confirm (Enter too) |\n| F | fend | dive tackle | |\n| C | kick (tap / hold; drop goal in their 22) | switch to the player nearest the ball | |\n| R | super | super | |\n| Esc / P | pause | pause | back |\n| Mouse move | hovering a teammate highlights him and shows whether a pass to him is legal (green) or forward (red) | hovering a teammate highlights him | hover moves the focus |\n| Left click | pass to the hovered teammate | take control of the hovered teammate | pick |\n| Right button (hold, let go) | kick toward the cursor: a landing ring follows the mouse, the meter fills toward it | | |\n| Wheel / - = | camera zoom (engine) | | scroll |\n| V | camera view | | |\n| ` (backquote) | dev panel, once Dev Mode is on (§14) | | |\n| Set pieces | Space times and mashes; Q/E alternate (scrum) or pick a pod (also W/S, or click a pod); set kicks: the mouse aims, hold the left button for power | | |\n\nThe mouse is native, not a stand-in: clicking a teammate passes or switches, the cursor aims kicks, hover focuses every\nmenu and card, clicking a lineout pod picks it, and set kicks aim and power with the mouse.\n\n### 6.3 Presets and two players on one keyboard\n\nOne preset object `KEYS`, built as the sports kit builds its own: start from `E.Input.DEFAULT` (its moves and its menu\nactions `start pause confirm cancel`), delete `attack dash skill jump`, add `x, sq, ci, tri, l1, r1, turbo` with the\ncodes in the tables above (pad codes: `Pad0` ✕, `Pad1` ○, `Pad2` □, `Pad3` △, `Pad4` L1, `Pad5` R1, `Pad7` R2,\n`Pad9` Options, `Pad11` R3; L2 is left free). Use it on every play scene (`input: KEYS`). `KEYS.halves` (patch 2):\n\n| Half | Move | Turbo | ✕ | □ | ○ | △ | L1 | R1 | Start / pause |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| left (P1) | WASD | LeftShift | Space | F | C | R | Q | E | 1 / Esc |\n| right (P2) | arrows | Comma, Numpad0 | Period, Numpad1 | Slash, Numpad2 | Semicolon, Numpad3 | Quote, Numpad6 | KeyK, Numpad4 | KeyL, Numpad5 | 2, Enter / Backspace |\n\n`menu: { controls: { x: 'Sidestep / Tackle', sq: 'Fend / Dive tackle', ci: 'Kick / Switch to ball', tri: 'Super',\nl1: 'Pass up / Switch up', r1: 'Pass down / Switch down', turbo: 'Turbo' } }` names them on the Controls screen; every\none is rebindable there.\n\n### 6.4 Prompts and glyphs follow the last device\n\n- Every prompt is built from actions, never literal keys: `TT.glyph(g, action, x, y, input)` draws a badge for the\n  device in `input.lastDevice` (`'gamepad'` → a pad badge in the chosen family; `'keyboard'` → a keycap with\n  `input.codeLabel(code)`; `'mouse'` → a mouse icon with the button lit when the action has a mouse binding, else the\n  keycap). Text prompts use markup `\"{b:x} TACKLE  {b:ci} SWITCH\"` parsed by `TT.prompt()`.\n- Override the standard `glyphs` setting from the game: `{ key: 'glyphs', group: 'Display', label: 'Controller\n  buttons', type: 'select', value: 'playstation', options: [['playstation', 'PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △'], ['xbox', 'Xbox A B X\n  Y']], apply: (g, v) => g.players.forEach(p => p.glyphs = v) }`, and set `input.glyphs` on each joiner. It then shows\n  in Settings, Display (the settings screen), where TASK.md wants it.\n- Badges: PlayStation ✕ blue `#6a9cf0`, ○ red `#f05a64`, □ pink `#e07ac8`, △ green `#4ad0a0` on a dark disc; L1/R1/R2\n  as rounded pills. Xbox A green `#5ac85a`, B red `#e8503a`, X blue `#3a86e8`, Y yellow `#f0c830`; LB/RB/RT pills.\n  Keycaps light grey with a dark shadow, wide for Space and Shift. All string sprites designed at 9-11 px, drawn at\n  scale 1 in the HUD (scale 2 on big prompts).\n\n### 6.5 Assists\n\nSettings, Game: **Auto-switch** (Assisted: to the defender nearest the ball each time the ball is passed or kicked, and\non ✕ when no one is in range; Full: always the best defender; Manual: only by buttons and mouse). **Pass assist** (On:\na pass button with no legal receiver on that side throws to the nearest legal one on the other side; Off: it is a\ndummy, §7.4). **Hold or toggle** turbo.\n\n### 6.6 Touch-only devices\n\n- Detect: `matchMedia('(any-pointer: coarse)').matches && !matchMedia('(any-pointer: fine)').matches` and no gamepad\n  has been seen and no key pressed. Re-check on `gamepadconnected`, `keydown` and `pointermove` with a mouse: any of\n  them leaves touch-only mode at once.\n- In touch-only mode: no touch buttons or stick anywhere (`game.input.touchEnabled = false`, every scene `touch: []`);\n  the title shows **CONTROLLER OR KEYBOARD REQUIRED** (scale 2) with \"Connect a controller or a keyboard to play.\" and a\n  big **SETTINGS** button (tap → `game.menu('settings')`); the attract mode still starts after 10 s; a tap during it\n  goes back to the title.\n\n---\n\n## 7. Match systems (rules and numbers)\n\nAll numbers are `TUNE` defaults (§14.2). \"u\" = world units.\n\n### 7.1 Match flow and clock\n\nPhases (`state.phase`), each with a watchdog (any phase longer than its limit resolves itself and emits a `watchdog`\nevent that tests fail on):\n\n```","numLines":470,"startLine":1,"totalLines":1679}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01KjHXws4C9tXbTNUxC6WEaW","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
