{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Y164xrEQzV7JVT88NSvMsX","type":"tool_result","content":"1320\t\n1321\t### 14.1 The panel\n1322\t\n1323\t- Pause menu → **DEV MODE** (in every pausable scene: matches and drills) turns dev mode on for the visit and opens\n1324\t  the panel over the play. After that, ` (backquote) toggles it from the keyboard. Its JUMP tab reaches every other\n1325\t  part of the game.\n1326\t- A canvas panel, 10-foot sized (scale 1 rows, eight rows visible, scrolling), docked on the right 45% of the screen.\n1327\t  Tabs: **TUNING · CHEATS · EVENTS · VIEW · JUMP** (L1/R1 or Q/E or click). D-pad or stick moves the focus; left/right\n1328\t  changes a slider (held: repeats and speeds up); ✕ toggles or fires; □ resets the focused slider; △ switches\n1329\t  **FROZEN** (the match stopped, `timeScale` 0, the default) and **LIVE** (the match runs; the panel stays and takes the\n1330\t  mouse: hover focuses, drag a slider bar, click a tab, wheel scrolls); ○ / Esc closes.\n1331\t- Every change applies at once to the running match (the simulation reads `TUNE.v`), and **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits\n1332\t  at the top of TUNING (with a confirm press). Values are kept for the visit (and in `E.store` `thugby:tune` so a\n1333\t  reload keeps them; the reset clears it).\n1334\t\n1335\t### 14.2 TUNING (the most important gameplay variables)\n1336\t\n1337\t| Key | Label | Default | Min | Max | Step |\n1338\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n1339\t| `runSpeed` | Run speed | 100 | 60 | 160 | 5 |\n1340\t| `turboMult` | Turbo multiplier | 1.45 | 1 | 2.2 | .05 |\n1341\t| `turboTank` | Turbo tank (s) | 3 | 1 | 8 | .25 |\n1342\t| `turboRegen` | Turbo refill (/s) | .3 | .05 | 1 | .05 |\n1343\t| `accel` | Acceleration | 850 | 300 | 2000 | 50 |\n1344\t| `passSpeed` | Pass speed | 230 | 120 | 400 | 10 |\n1345\t| `catchBase` | Catch chance | .95 | .5 | 1 | .01 |\n1346\t| `pressurePenalty` | Catch pressure penalty | .12 | 0 | .4 | .01 |\n1347\t| `tackleRange` | Tackle reach | 14 | 8 | 30 | 1 |\n1348\t| `tackleBase` | Tackle success | .78 | .3 | 1 | .01 |\n1349\t| `bigHitCharge` | Big hit charge time (s) | .7 | .2 | 2 | .05 |\n1350\t| `bigHitLaunch` | Big hit launch | 1 | .2 | 3 | .1 |\n1351\t| `spillChance` | Monster hit spill chance | .5 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n1352\t| `fendWindow` | Fend window (s) | .3 | .05 | .8 | .05 |\n1353\t| `fendBeat` | Fend strength | .7 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n1354\t| `sidestepPower` | Sidestep power | 140 | 40 | 300 | 10 |\n1355\t| `sidestepEvade` | Sidestep evade (s) | .25 | .05 | .6 | .05 |\n1356\t| `diveRange` | Dive tackle reach | 30 | 10 | 60 | 2 |\n1357\t| `kickMax` | Kick distance | 520 | 200 | 900 | 20 |\n1358\t| `windMult` | Wind strength | 1 | 0 | 3 | .1 |\n1359\t| `bounceChaos` | Oval bounce chaos | .6 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n1360\t| `ruckTime` | Ruck length (s) | 1.6 | .5 | 4 | .1 |\n1361\t| `mashWeight` | Ruck mash weight | .05 | .01 | .12 | .005 |\n1362\t| `scrumPushWeight` | Scrum push weight | .05 | .01 | .15 | .005 |\n1363\t| `hypeMult` | Hype gain | 1 | 0 | 3 | .1 |\n1364\t| `superTime` | Super length (s) | 4 | 1 | 10 | .5 |\n1365\t| `powerupEvery` | Power-up every (s) | 30 | 5 | 120 | 5 |\n1366\t| `hazardEvery` | Hazard frequency | 1 | 0 | 3 | .1 |\n1367\t| `aiReaction` | CPU reaction (s) | .25 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n1368\t| `aiAggression` | CPU aggression | .5 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n1369\t| `cpuMash` | CPU mash rate (/s) | 9 | 2 | 20 | 1 |\n1370\t| `refRange` | Ref sight range | 260 | 60 | 600 | 10 |\n1371\t| `refFov` | Ref sight angle (rad) | 1.4 | .4 | 3.14 | .05 |\n1372\t| `sinBin` | Sin bin (s) | 30 | 5 | 120 | 5 |\n1373\t| `halfLength` | Half length (s) | 120 | 20 | 600 | 10 |\n1374\t| `gameSpeed` | Game speed | 1 | .25 | 2 | .05 |\n1375\t\n1376\t### 14.3 CHEATS (toggles)\n1377\t\n1378\tGod mode (my team cannot be tackled), infinite turbo, hype always full, always ON FIRE, ref blind (sees nothing), ref\n1379\tstrict (sees everything), CPU frozen, no clock, big heads (`headR` × 1.8 on every rig).\n1380\t\n1381\t### 14.4 EVENTS (one press each; the same `game.debug.force(name)` the attract director and tests use)\n1382\t\n1383\tScore a try (home / away), give P1 the ball, ball to the opponents' 5 m line, knock-on → scrum, touch → lineout, foul →\n1384\tpenalty (then the choice), yellow card, kick-off, 22 drop-out, a ruck now, monster hit on the carrier, spawn each\n1385\tpower-up, trigger the venue's hazard (and each other venue's), ON FIRE for P1's player, Thunder Run, Hurricane Hit,\n1386\treplay the last 5 s, the hooter, skip to halftime, skip to full time, sudden death, set score (+5 / -5 each team),\n1387\tset clock (-30 s).\n1388\t\n1389\t### 14.5 VIEW (debug overlays) and JUMP\n1390\t\n1391\tVIEW: AI shape slots and roles, the referee's cone (always), offside lines (always), tackle and fend ranges, pass lanes\n1392\tand legal receivers, rig skeletons (`rig.debug`), the engine's frame-rate readout, slow motion (0.25x), frame step (□\n1393\tadvances one step while FROZEN), state readout (phase, timers, possession). JUMP: any cup round's match or cutscene, any\n1394\tdrill, the victory, the third half, the credits, a session-style match (halves 75 s, no replays).\n1395\t\n1396\t---\n1397\t\n1398\t## 15. Multiplayer\n1399\t\n1400\t### 15.1 On its own: local play (1-4 on one machine)\n1401\t\n1402\t- `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })` on the join screen: Start on a free pad, or 2 for the keyboard's right half\n1403\t  (patch 2's layouts). Each seat picks HOME, AWAY or CPU; in the cup everyone is on the Mudlarks (co-op).\n1404\t- One shared screen (the broadcast camera follows the ball; nobody needs their own view). Each seat has its colour\n1405\t  (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`), ring, chevron, HUD card and its own glyphs (its own device).\n1406\t- Co-op (all humans on one team vs CPU) and versus (any split, CPU filling the rest). Each human controls one player at\n1407\t  a time (§7.2). The pause says who paused (engine).\n1408\t\n1409\t### 15.2 AI Hub sessions (the protocol at lib/hub/README.md)\n1410\t\n1411\t**Mode: `shared-world`, 2-4 players.** One match, the host's copy runs it (all 14 players' rules, contact, AI, ball,\n1412\treferee); every other copy sends what its player does and draws what the host sends. It is the most fun mode for a team\n1413\tsport and the architecture (§4.1) is built for it. **Fallback:** if the 4-player lossy harness run still fails at the\n1414\tend of M8, switch the declared mode to `parallel` (every player plays the same seeded 3-minute match against the CPU;\n1415\t`progress` and `result({ score: points for - points against + 100 })`), which the same architecture supports with no\n1416\tnetwork code; record the decision in the About screen.\n1417\t\n1418\t**Tags in `<head>`** (after the session client):\n1419\t\n1420\t```html\n1421\t<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n1422\t<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n1423\t<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n1424\t<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n1425\t<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Score more points than the other team\">\n1426\t<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick/WASD run · L1/R1 or Q/E pass · ✕/Space tackle or step · ○/C kick\">\n1427\t<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n1428\t<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n1429\t```\n1430\t\n1431\t**Flow:**\n1432\t1. At load, `AIHub.active` (true from the first line) builds the game with session options: `pausable: false` on the\n1433\t   match, `autoPause: false`, `corner: false`, `fullscreen: false` (the hub owns full screen), `menu: { quit: false,\n1434\t   items: [] }`, no title, no attract, no saves, no records, no dev mode, no replays, no cutscenes, no pause.\n1435\t2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [2, 4], card: true })`; `null` → the normal game\n1436\t   (title).\n1437\t3. Setup from `AIHub.rng('setup')` (the same on every copy, no messages needed): the home side is the Mudlarks, the away\n1438\t   side one of the four rivals, the venue one of four, the weather the venue's. Seats: even ids HOME, odd AWAY (2\n1439\t   players 1v1, 3 players 2v1, 4 players 2v2); CPU fills the rest. Halves: `min(75, (limitMs / 1000 - 40) / 2.6)` s\n1440\t   of running clock (75 when `limitMs` is 0); no sudden death (a draw is a tie).\n1441\t4. Build the match (boot steps), then show **our card** (scene `card`) and call `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press.\n1442\t   The card: the venue at dusk, both teams lined up, each human's player in front with the hub name in the seat colour\n1443\t   (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) and a READY tick from the `card` event's `ready`; \"HOW TO WIN: score more\n1444\t   points than the other team\"; this player's controls as badges for its device (five lines at most); the countdown\n1445\t   from `card.left` (\"STARTS IN 12\"), \"PAUSED: players asked for more time\" while `paused`; the title music. Enter or ✕\n1446\t   (button 0) or a tap on READY → `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △ (button 3), M, or a tap on MORE TIME →\n1447\t   `AIHub.answer('more')`. Nothing on the card draws from `Math.random` (use `AIHub.rng('card')`).\n1448\t5. `start` → a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0` (big numbers over the kick-off), the whistle at 0, play.\n1449\t6. Full time (host): `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })`: standings `[[winning side's ids], [losing\n1450\t   side's ids]]` or `[[everyone]]` for a draw; scores `{ id: their side's points }`; stats per id (tries, tackles, big\n1451\t   hits, metres); awards (Player of the Match, Biggest Hit). A last reliable snapshot `{ over: 1 }` first.\n1452\t7. `time-up` → the host finishes at once with the current score (within `graceMs`).\n1453\t8. `leave` → that seat's player goes back to the CPU; if a side has no humans left the match still runs to full time.\n1454\t9. `end` → stop simulation, input and sound (`audio.stopMusic`, mute), show \"FULL TIME\" under the hub's results.\n1455\t10. `AIHub.server` (a host with no player): run the same host code with no local seat; `draw` returns at once.\n1456\t\n1457\t**Messages** (JSON; at most 20 a second leave a copy, bursts to 40; unreliable ones under about 1,200 bytes):\n1458\t\n1459\t| Message | Direction | Reliable | Rate | Contents |\n1460\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n1461\t| `i` (intent) | client → host | no | 15/s | `{ t: 'i', s: seq, at: AIHub.time(), m: [mx, my] (×100 ints), h: held bits, p: [[action, at, id], ...] (presses of the last 1 s, deduplicated by id), pos: [x, y] when its player is free-running }` |\n1462\t| `s` (snapshot) | host → all | no | 12/s | `{ t: 's', at, ph: phase code, c: clock×10, sc: [h, a], hy: [h, a], b: [x, y, z, state, holder], r: [x, y, f, down], P: [[id, x, y, z, f×100, st, ctl, anim...], ...14] }`, ints only, anim fields only when not default |\n1463\t| `e` (events) | host → all | yes | ≤ 5/s, bundled every 200 ms | `{ t: 'e', l: [[id, name, data], ...] }`: scores, phase changes, set-piece setups (with their parameters and timestamps), cards, banners, power-ups, hazards, commentary keys, `over` |\n1464\t| `ctl` | host → one | yes | on change | which on-field player the seat now drives |\n1465\t\n1466\t- **Client-side feel:** like the hub's arena example, each copy moves its own controlled player at once from its own\n1467\t  stick while that player is free-running (`idle/run/carry/charge`) and reports `pos`; the host takes that position\n1468\t  (clamped to what the player could have run since the last report, +20 u) and decides everything else: contact, the\n1469\t  ball, the laws. When the host puts the player into any other state (tackled, ruck, scrum...), the client follows the\n1470\t  host's state and eases its drawn position to the snapshot's.\n1471\t- **Everyone else on a client** is drawn from snapshots interpolated 120 ms behind (positions lerp, facing by the short\n1472\t  arc, discrete states switch at snapshot boundaries); the same presentation code as live play draws them (rigs from\n1473\t  the `anim` record, §11.1). Events trigger the same commentary, sound, banners and particles locally. No snapshot for\n1474\t  1 s: a small \"WAITING FOR THE HOST...\" note.\n1475\t- **Timing mini-games online:** the host stamps each set piece's key moment (scrum SET, lineout throw arrival, kick-off\n1476\t  catch) with `AIHub.time()` in its event; clients show the cue against their own `AIHub.time()` and send their press\n1477\t  with its `at`; the host judges the press by `|at - T|` (the hub keeps clocks within about 15 ms).\n1478\t- **Snapshot budget:** 99% of snapshots ≤ 1,100 bytes (tested, §19.3); if a build exceeds it, send players 0-6 and 7-13\n1479\t  in alternate snapshots.\n1480\t- **Rules in a session:** no network but the hub; no chat, emotes or custom names (names from the hub, never inserted\n1481\t  as HTML: the pixel font draws them, accents stripped with `normalize('NFD')` and any other character it lacks shown\n1482\t  as a dot); no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no title, restart\n1483\t  or quit; Options is ignored while Create is held; no hit-stop (`game.freeze` would stall the host's sends: hits use\n1484\t  `particles.impact`, sparks, shake and a sound instead, and each copy may hold its drawn frame for 60 ms); nothing\n1485\t  outside a session changes.\n1486\t\n1487\t---\n1488\t\n1489\t## 16. Settings and accessibility (own settings via `menu.settings`)\n1490\t\n1491\t| Key | Group | Label | Options (default first) |\n1492\t|---|---|---|---|\n1493\t| `glyphs` (override) | Display | Controller buttons | PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ · Xbox A B X Y |\n1494\t| `uiScale` | Display | TV text size | Large · Normal (Large raises HUD prompts to scale 2 and the shell CSS above) |\n1495\t| `camera` | Display | Camera | Broadcast · High · Overhead (the views of §5.3; R3 and V cycle them in play) |\n1496\t| `difficulty` | Game | Difficulty | Pro · Rookie · Legend |\n1497\t| `halves` | Game | Half length | 2 min · 3 · 4 · 5 |\n1498\t| `suddenDeath` | Game | Sudden death in Exhibition | On · Off |\n1499\t| `autoSwitch` | Game | Auto-switch | Assisted · Full · Manual |\n1500\t| `passAssist` | Game | Pass assist | On · Off |\n1501\t| `turboMode` | Game | Turbo | Hold · Toggle |\n1502\t| `commentary` | Game | Commentary | On · Text only · Off |\n1503\t| `replays` | Game | Replays | Big moments · All tries · Off |\n1504\t| `refCone` | Game | Show the ref's view | On · Off |\n1505\t| `bigHeads` | Game | Big heads (after the cup) | Off · On |\n1506\t| `crowd` | Sound | Crowd volume | slider .8 |\n1507\t\n1508\tThe engine's standard settings stay (volume, music, effects, reduce motion, shake, flashes, pixel scaling, filter,\n1509\tframe rate) and are honoured: shake, flash and zoom punches go through `game.shake` / `game.flash` / tweens that check\n1510\t`game.reduceMotion`. Information is never in colour alone: teams have crests and three-letter names, seats have labels,\n1511\tlines have words.\n1512\t\n1513\t---\n1514\t\n1515\t## 17. Build order and milestones\n1516\t\n1517\tEach milestone ends with its tests passing (`node test/run.mjs`) and, where it says so, screenshots looked at. Do the\n1518\triskiest work first (M1-M2.5).\n1519\t\n1520\t| M | Build | Exit criteria |\n1521\t|---|---|---|\n1522\t| **M0 Scaffold** | the dev folder; copy the engine, tools and `package.json` (`npm install`; Playwright's Chromium is already cached on this machine at `~/.cache/ms-playwright`, else `npx playwright install chromium`); fetch `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` (`curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>`); `build.mjs`, `page.html` with the hub tags; the vm loader and `test/run.mjs`; a placeholder-free stub (a title scene with the logo). Practical notes: `/tmp` on this machine was full during planning, so keep scratch files in the dev folder; take the engine's `dist/handoff` with `git clone --depth 1` of my-3d2dge (about 150 MB) and the hub files with `curl` (the ai-hub repository is over 5 GB: do not clone it) | `node test/run.mjs` prints `N passed, 0 failed`; the built HTML passes `tools/check.mjs`; a test asserts the session client is the first script in `<head>` and unmodified |\n1523\t| **M1 Pitch, players, ball (spikes)** | geometry, TileMap, `floorTex` lines, posts, stands, boards; the `broadcast` view; 14 dressed Humanoids in formation; one human-controlled player with turbo; the carry pose; the loose oval ball, pick-up; the camera; the radar. **Spikes:** a superman dive (`down: 1` at height) and the tackle as a paired `E.Action`; `r.pick` on a teammate; 14 rigs + crowd on screen | ball physics tests; screenshots of the pitch in three views at 1280x720 and 1920x1080 look like a broadcast; the dive and tackle filmstrips read |\n1524\t| **M2 Core loop** | passing (L1/R1, legal receivers, catches, knock-ons), the tackle → simple ruck → recycle, tries and grounding, conversions (simple), kick-off (simple), the clock, halves, hooter, full time; attack and defence shapes; auto-switch; `MatchState` and intents in place | a CPU v CPU match plays to full time with scores and no stalls (watchdog never fires); a scripted human scores a try |\n1525\t| **M2.5 Net spike** | `MatchState` snapshot encoding; the fake hub (in-process, latency/jitter/loss); host + one client in Node: the client's intents move its player, its snapshots draw | the loopback test passes; snapshot size test passes |\n1526\t| **M3 Laws and set pieces** | forward pass, knock-on → scrum; touch → lineout; set kicks with the kicker camera (yaw spike); penalties and the choice; free kicks; drop goals; 22 drop-outs; the referee (runs, sees, calls, signals); offside lines; advantage; cards and the sin bin; penalty try; \"use it\" | law and set-piece tests; CPU v CPU matches visit every set piece (coverage) |\n1527\t| **M4 Arcade layer** | sidestep, spin, fend, dummy, offload, interceptions; big and monster hits; dive and gang tackles; cheap shots; hype and supers; ON FIRE; power-ups; the four hazards and weather; ref down; instant replay | a test per system; across 20 seeded CPU matches with forcing, every event fires |\n1528\t| **M5 Presentation** | the full HUD with badges; commentary and babble (patch 4); music and SFX; crowd; lights; effects; every animation in §11.2 polished; venue dressing (boards, floodlights, jumbotron, skylines); patches 1 and 3 | `tools/check.mjs` look notes acted on; screenshots of each venue, set piece and HUD state reviewed; filmstrips of every move reviewed |\n1529\t| **M6 Modes and content** | title, menu, cup hub, the story scenes and sets, halftime talks, results, victory, third half, credits; Exhibition setup; the join screen and local multiplayer (patch 2); the Training Ground's nine drills with SHOW ME; records and initials; About & Credits; saves and CONTINUE; unlocks | the scripted full playthrough passes (§19.4); save and continue tests pass |\n1530\t| **M7 Platform** | the attract mode and its director; touch-only mode; dev mode (panel, tuning, cheats, events, view, jump, hotkeys); own settings and the glyph override; the TV CSS for the shell; device-following prompts everywhere | attract, dev, glyph and touch-only tests pass |\n1531\t| **M8 AI Hub** | session options; connect; our card; host, client and server paths; intents, snapshots and events; finish, time-up, leave, end | the loopback suite (4 players, 150 ms ± 50, 5% loss; host leave; server) passes; the real harness passes in all four configurations (§19.6); standalone still plays |\n1532\t| **M9 Balance, polish, ship** | tune with the dev panel to the targets in §19.7; difficulty through the cup; a bug bash; final About text and exit interview | every test passes; the final HTML checked in Chromium (no errors); the About screen's text is real; `exit-interview.json` parses |\n1533\t\n1534\t---\n1535\t\n1536\t## 18. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1537\t\n1538\t| Risk | Why it is risky | De-risk |\n1539\t|---|---|---|\n1540\t| The AI plays like bees round a jam pot | 14 players chasing the ball look nothing like rugby | role slots and \"only two chase\" (§7.19) from M2; metric tests (defensive line spread ≥ 300 u across 6 players, support depth behind the carrier, no more than 3 players within 40 u of the ball in open play 90% of the time) |\n1541\t| Phases that never end | a ruck with nobody committed, a ball stuck under a pile, a set piece waiting forever | a watchdog on every phase (§7.1); tests fail on any watchdog; the attract director doubles as a soak test |\n1542\t| Tackles, dives and scrums look wrong | rigs are procedural; flat dives and binds are new | M1 spikes; paired `E.ACTION`s; filmstrip reviews; fallbacks: the engine's `down` and `getUp`, `crouch` and `lean` alone |\n1543\t| Clutter at 427x240 with 15 rigs and a crowd | the player loses the ball and himself | contrasting kits (luminance rule), seat rings, x-ray outlines, the ball carrier outline, zoom .9, the radar, off-screen arrows; screenshot review in M1 and M5 |\n1544\t| The backwards-pass rule frustrates | new players pass forward and get whistled | receivers are chosen behind by default; the L1/R1 badges show only legal receivers; the mouse hover shows red for forward; drill 2 teaches it |\n1545\t| Online shared world | lag, loss, a contact sport, timing mini-games | the state/presentation split from M2; the M2.5 spike; client-moved own player; time-stamped presses; ints in snapshots; the loopback suite; no replays or cutscenes online; fallback to `parallel` (§15.2) |\n1546\t| Hub compliance traps | the engine pauses, quits, goes full screen and stores by default | the session option list (§15.2); harness runs early in M8 (a minimal card can be tried at M2.5) |\n1547\t| Glyphs and fonts | the font lacks PS symbols; pads report odd names | patch 1; badges; label tests with `input.glyphs` both ways |\n1548\t| Determinism and flaky tests | randomness and timing | `TT.rng` only in the simulation; fixed seeds in tests; no wall clock in the simulation |\n1549\t| The single-file bundle | script order, the session client first, `</script>` in strings | `build.mjs` checks; a test parses the final HTML; `tools/check.mjs` on the final file |\n1550\t| Slow headless tests | rigs cost time | `TT.cfg.present = false` headless |\n1551\t| Scope | a lot of content | the order above makes the game whole by M4; content after; every system stays even if story lines get shorter |\n1552\t| Touch-only false positives | touch laptops | require no fine pointer; leave the mode on any key, mouse or pad |\n1553\t\n1554\t---\n1555\t\n1556\t## 19. Testing\n1557\t\n1558\t### 19.1 One command\n1559\t\n1560\t`node test/run.mjs` (from the dev folder) rebuilds, runs every headless suite, and prints **only failures** (`FAIL\n1561\tsuite › case: message`) and **one line**: `N passed, M failed (S suites, T s)`; exit code 1 on any failure. `--only\n1562\t<suite>` reruns one suite (rerun a suite only after changing the code it covers). `--browser` adds the Playwright\n1563\tchecks, `--hub` the AI Hub harness runs (both print the same way). Tests are scripts that play the game and check its\n1564\tstate through `game.advance` and `game.debug`; screenshots only for what a script cannot check; no subagents or\n1565\tworkflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\n1566\t\n1567\t### 19.2 The harness\n1568\t\n1569\t`test/lib/load.mjs` creates a `vm` context with stand-ins (`window` = the context, `addEventListener`,\n1570\t`removeEventListener`, `location { hash, search }`, `matchMedia(() => ({ matches: false }))`, `navigator {\n1571\tgetGamepads: () => [] }`, `performance`, a quiet `console`), runs `engine/my-3d2dge.js` then `build/game.js`, and\n1572\treturns `{ game, TT, E }` (no DOM, so the engine is headless). Helpers: `snap()`, `advance(s)`, `tap(action, seat)`,\n1573\t`hold(action, on, seat)`, `feed(pointer events)`, `untilPhase(name, maxS)`, `playMatch(opts)` (CPU v CPU or autopilot),\n1574\t`fakeHub(n, { latency, jitter, loss, server, hostLeave })` (several contexts wired through an in-process network with\n1575\t`AIHub` stand-ins that implement `connect`, `ready`, `answer`, `time`, `on`, `send`, `finish`, `rng`, `active`).\n1576\t\n1577\t### 19.3 Suites (what each checks)\n1578\t\n1579\t| Suite | Checks |\n1580\t|---|---|\n1581\t| `boot` | boots headless with no errors; every scene enters and exits cleanly; `game.debug.snapshot()` fields present; the final HTML has the session client first, unmodified, and every hub tag |\n1582\t| `ball` | pass arcs land within 4 u of the lead point; punts land within 5% of their predicted spot without wind; the oval bounce stays within its angle and height bounds and is the same for the same seed; touch at the right x; dead-ball → drop-out |\n1583\t| `laws` | backward and flat passes legal; forward (relative to the passer) whistled; a sprinting lateral pass legal; knock-on → scrum to the other team; tries only when grounded in the in-goal; 5/2/3/3/7 scoring; conversion geometry (in line with the grounding); the hooter waits for a stoppage; sudden death ends on the first score |\n1584\t| `contact` | tackle success over 400 seeded tries within ±5% of the formula; big hits launch beyond 30 u; the fend window beats tackles at the expected rate; a sidestep makes tackles whiff; BIG UNIT needs two tacklers; a cheap shot seen (ref facing) is a penalty and unseen (ref turned away) is not |\n1585\t| `breakdown` | ruck results match §7.8's worked example (two attackers against one: a human at 0/s loses to a CPU at 9/s, at 12/s beats a CPU at 6/s); jackal swing; hands in the ruck; \"use it\" → scrum; offside flagged only when seen |\n1586\t| `setpieces` | scrum timing windows (perfect, good, early engagement → free kick); push outcomes; pushover try; lineout calls, steals and not straight; kick-off ten metres; shot clock; penalty choices each lead to the right restart |\n1587\t| `arcade` | hype gains per event; each super starts, works and ends; ON FIRE starts on the second try and ends on the opponents' score; each power-up spawns, is taken and expires; each hazard warns then strikes and drops a carrier's ball; ref down silences fouls for 8 s |\n1588\t| `ai` | 30 seeded CPU v CPU matches (30 s halves): no watchdog, no errors, scores 0-80 a side, tries per match 2-14 on average 4-9, both teams pass, tackle, kick, ruck and scrum; shape metrics (§18); the AI never stands offside at a set piece |\n1589\t| `control` | auto-switch modes; L1/R1 and ○ switching; mouse click on a teammate passes or switches; a seat never takes a player held by another seat |\n1590\t| `input` | `label()` gives ✕ ○ □ △ by default on a pad, A B X Y after `settings.set('glyphs', 'xbox')`, keys on the keyboard; prompts change with `lastDevice`; half-keyboard layouts (patch 2) map as §6.3; the font draws ✕ ○ □ △ (patch 1: no `?` glyph used) |\n1591\t| `replay` | the ring buffer holds 5 s; a replay plays and the match resumes exactly where it was |\n1592\t| `proof` | the kit-style proof: a scripted 600-step match from seed 7 gives the same hash twice |\n1593\t| `drills` | each drill completes with its `auto` script; each one's failure path resets |\n1594\t| `attract` | 10 s of idle on the title starts it; a 4-minute attract loop fires every showcase step once; a key, a pad button and a mouse move each return to the title without picking anything; touch-only mode shows the message and still runs it |\n1595\t| `dev` | DEV MODE from the pause menu opens the panel; each slider changes `TUNE.v` and the simulation (run speed → measured speed); reset restores every default; each cheat and event works |\n1596\t| `saves` | the cup autosaves; CONTINUE resumes at the right round; records and unlocks persist; a bad save falls back |\n1597\t| `local` | four seats join (pads and the right keyboard half); 2v2 and co-op matches run with each seat driving its own player |\n1598\t| `net` | loopback: 2 players clean; 4 players at 150 ± 50 ms and 5% loss for 60 s of play; clients' players move from their intents; snapshots ≤ 1,100 bytes 99% of the time and under 12/s; events arrive once; `finish` once with valid standings at full time; `time-up` finishes within grace; a leaver's player goes CPU; host leave ends cleanly; server mode runs with no local seat; card answers ready on Enter; no `Math.random` use on the card |\n1599\t\n1600\t### 19.4 The scripted playthrough of the whole game\n1601\t\n1602\t`playthrough.test.mjs`: title → PRESS START → menu → MEGA CUP → prologue (pages turned) → TRAIN → each of the nine\n1603\tdrills by its `auto` script → pre-match scene → round one played honestly by the autopilot (the human seat driven by the\n1604\tCPU brain through the intent interface, like the sports kit's `bot()`; halves 30 s) until won (retry with CONTINUE if\n1605\tlost; the test fails after three losses) → results → cup hub (autosave checked) → quarter-final, semi-final and grand\n1606\tfinal (halves 30 s; god mode allowed for the final only, so the boss's gang-tackle rule is still exercised by the CPU)\n1607\t→ victory → third half → credits → title. It asserts the scene order, the saves, the unlocks, and no errors or\n1608\twatchdogs anywhere.\n1609\t\n1610\t### 19.5 In a browser (what scripts cannot check)\n1611\t\n1612\t- `node tools/check.mjs <final html>` (and `#scene` deep links: `#match`, `#cup`, `#training`, `#attract`): no errors,\n1613\t  look notes acted on; `--size 1920x1080` and `--size 390x844@3` (touch-only screen).\n1614\t- Screenshots to look at (once per milestone that changes them): each venue in each view; kick-off, ruck, scrum,\n1615\t  lineout, set kick, penalty choice; a monster hit; a replay; the HUD with 1 and 4 seats; each menu screen; the dev\n1616\t  panel; the hub card; results; credits.\n1617\t- Filmstrips (`tools/filmstrip.mjs --seed 1`) of every move in §11.2.\n1618\t- A pad by hand if one is available (else `input.press('Pad0')` scripted in Playwright).\n1619\t\n1620\t### 19.6 The AI Hub harness\n1621\t\n1622\tServe the dev folder's `hub/` (holding the harness files and a copy of the built HTML) with `python3 -m http.server 0\n1623\t--bind 127.0.0.1` (note the port it prints and its PID), fetch the copied HTML from that port and check its `<title>`\n1624\tis \"Thugby Turbo\" (another agent's server may hold a port: never trust a page you have not checked), then with\n1625\tPlaywright open `harness.html?game=<html>&players=2&auto=1`; `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`;\n1626\tthe same with `&scenario=host-leave`; and `&players=2&scenario=server`. Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete ===\n1627\t'true'`; pass when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true; print failing checks only. Stop only the server you started, by its\n1628\tPID. Then open the HTML on its own to confirm it still plays outside a session.\n1629\t\n1630\t### 19.7 Balance targets (tuned with the dev panel, measured by the `ai` suite and by hand)\n1631\t\n1632\tA Pro v Pro CPU match: 4-9 tries, 60% of conversions, 1-3 monster hits, 2-4 scrums, 2-4 lineouts, a card every two or\n1633\tthree matches, a power-up taken every 40 s. A new human on Pro wins round one about four times in five and loses the\n1634\tfinal at the first try about half the time. Turbo runs out in about one long break. A big hit should feel like an event,\n1635\tnot every tackle.\n1636\t\n1637\t---\n1638\t\n1639\t## 20. About & Credits screen and the exit interview\n1640\t\n1641\t- **About & Credits** (`about` scene, five pages at scale 1-2, L1/R1 or left/right, ✕ closes):\n1642\t  1. THUGBY TURBO: made with the my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (`E.versionLabel()`), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0; by\n1643\t     the model that built it; the title's RNG pick; \"an original game inspired by rugby; every team, person and place\n1644\t     is made up\".\n1645\t  2. SOURCE INFORMATION: Date 2026-11-03 17:48:28 · Source: rugby (over-the-top arcade style) · Plan: PLAN.md · Genre\n1646\t     and perspective · Tech · Art direction · Animation · Audio · Online · Testing · Research · Environment · Model ·\n1647\t     Usage (estimates only if available; \"unknown\" otherwise).\n1648\t  3. TESTING FEEDBACK (the four answers). 4. ENGINE FEEDBACK (the six answers, including the patches and why). 5. PROMPT\n1649\t     FEEDBACK (the four answers).\n1650\t  The builder writes these from the real build at the end (M9); \"unknown\" for anything that can't be confirmed. The\n1651\t  same text goes in the final chat reply.\n1652\t- **`exit-interview.json`** in this folder, beside the HTML, in exactly TASK.md's shape; suggested `aiHub` values:\n1653\t  name \"THUGBY TURBO\", tagline \"Big hits, bigger hair, seven a side\", description \"Over-the-top arcade rugby sevens:\n1654\t  turbo runs, monster hits, a referee who only sees half of it, and a pub team's run at the Mega Cup.\", type \"Game\",\n1655\t  genre \"Sports\", source \"Rugby\", series \"\", players \"1–4\", controls \"Stick or WASD run, L1/R1 or Q/E pass, ✕ or Space\n1656\t  tackle and sidestep, ○ or C kick, R2 or Shift turbo, △ or R super\", tags `[\"sports\", \"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"pixel art\",\n1657\t  \"versus\", \"co-op\"]`. Check it parses (`node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`).\n1658\t\n1659\t---\n1660\t\n1661\t## 21. Definition of done\n1662\t\n1663\t- [ ] `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828.html` in this folder plays from `file://` with no errors;\n1664\t      the dev subfolder holds the sources, tests and tools.\n1665\t- [ ] Every system in §7 works in play and is shown by the cup, the drills or the attract mode; nothing is stubbed; no\n1666\t      TODO or placeholder text anywhere (grep the sources for TODO, FIXME, TBD, lorem, placeholder).\n1667\t- [ ] Mega Cup (prologue, training, four matches, victory, third half, credits), Exhibition (any teams, venues,\n1668\t      weather, 1-4 players), Training Ground, Records, Settings, About & Credits all reachable and complete.\n1669\t- [ ] Controller-first 10-foot UI; PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox in Settings; keyboard and mouse native; prompts\n1670\t      follow the last device; touch-only screen with Settings; attract mode after 10 s, any input exits.\n1671\t- [ ] Dev mode from the pause menu: live sliders for §14.2, reset to defaults, cheats, events, overlays, jumps.\n1672\t- [ ] All audio in code; babble voices for every speaker; music per scene with layers.\n1673\t- [ ] Local multiplayer 1-4; AI Hub shared-world passes the harness in all four configurations; outside a session the\n1674\t      game is unaffected.\n1675\t- [ ] `node test/run.mjs` passes, including the scripted playthrough; browser checks and screenshots reviewed.\n1676\t- [ ] Engine patches listed in `engine/PATCHES.md` and in Engine Feedback.\n1677\t- [ ] About & Credits pages and the chat reply carry Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback;\n1678\t      `exit-interview.json` parses.\n1679\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"bb1db6ef-667b-410d-be5b-5902ed56b3d4","timestamp":"2026-11-04T13:56:36.565Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-medium.build__20261104-065619__xa7e588/work/PLAN.md","content":"\n### 14.1 The panel\n\n- Pause menu → **DEV MODE** (in every pausable scene: matches and drills) turns dev mode on for the visit and opens\n  the panel over the play. After that, ` (backquote) toggles it from the keyboard. Its JUMP tab reaches every other\n  part of the game.\n- A canvas panel, 10-foot sized (scale 1 rows, eight rows visible, scrolling), docked on the right 45% of the screen.\n  Tabs: **TUNING · CHEATS · EVENTS · VIEW · JUMP** (L1/R1 or Q/E or click). D-pad or stick moves the focus; left/right\n  changes a slider (held: repeats and speeds up); ✕ toggles or fires; □ resets the focused slider; △ switches\n  **FROZEN** (the match stopped, `timeScale` 0, the default) and **LIVE** (the match runs; the panel stays and takes the\n  mouse: hover focuses, drag a slider bar, click a tab, wheel scrolls); ○ / Esc closes.\n- Every change applies at once to the running match (the simulation reads `TUNE.v`), and **RESET ALL TO DEFAULTS** sits\n  at the top of TUNING (with a confirm press). Values are kept for the visit (and in `E.store` `thugby:tune` so a\n  reload keeps them; the reset clears it).\n\n### 14.2 TUNING (the most important gameplay variables)\n\n| Key | Label | Default | Min | Max | Step |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `runSpeed` | Run speed | 100 | 60 | 160 | 5 |\n| `turboMult` | Turbo multiplier | 1.45 | 1 | 2.2 | .05 |\n| `turboTank` | Turbo tank (s) | 3 | 1 | 8 | .25 |\n| `turboRegen` | Turbo refill (/s) | .3 | .05 | 1 | .05 |\n| `accel` | Acceleration | 850 | 300 | 2000 | 50 |\n| `passSpeed` | Pass speed | 230 | 120 | 400 | 10 |\n| `catchBase` | Catch chance | .95 | .5 | 1 | .01 |\n| `pressurePenalty` | Catch pressure penalty | .12 | 0 | .4 | .01 |\n| `tackleRange` | Tackle reach | 14 | 8 | 30 | 1 |\n| `tackleBase` | Tackle success | .78 | .3 | 1 | .01 |\n| `bigHitCharge` | Big hit charge time (s) | .7 | .2 | 2 | .05 |\n| `bigHitLaunch` | Big hit launch | 1 | .2 | 3 | .1 |\n| `spillChance` | Monster hit spill chance | .5 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n| `fendWindow` | Fend window (s) | .3 | .05 | .8 | .05 |\n| `fendBeat` | Fend strength | .7 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n| `sidestepPower` | Sidestep power | 140 | 40 | 300 | 10 |\n| `sidestepEvade` | Sidestep evade (s) | .25 | .05 | .6 | .05 |\n| `diveRange` | Dive tackle reach | 30 | 10 | 60 | 2 |\n| `kickMax` | Kick distance | 520 | 200 | 900 | 20 |\n| `windMult` | Wind strength | 1 | 0 | 3 | .1 |\n| `bounceChaos` | Oval bounce chaos | .6 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n| `ruckTime` | Ruck length (s) | 1.6 | .5 | 4 | .1 |\n| `mashWeight` | Ruck mash weight | .05 | .01 | .12 | .005 |\n| `scrumPushWeight` | Scrum push weight | .05 | .01 | .15 | .005 |\n| `hypeMult` | Hype gain | 1 | 0 | 3 | .1 |\n| `superTime` | Super length (s) | 4 | 1 | 10 | .5 |\n| `powerupEvery` | Power-up every (s) | 30 | 5 | 120 | 5 |\n| `hazardEvery` | Hazard frequency | 1 | 0 | 3 | .1 |\n| `aiReaction` | CPU reaction (s) | .25 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n| `aiAggression` | CPU aggression | .5 | 0 | 1 | .05 |\n| `cpuMash` | CPU mash rate (/s) | 9 | 2 | 20 | 1 |\n| `refRange` | Ref sight range | 260 | 60 | 600 | 10 |\n| `refFov` | Ref sight angle (rad) | 1.4 | .4 | 3.14 | .05 |\n| `sinBin` | Sin bin (s) | 30 | 5 | 120 | 5 |\n| `halfLength` | Half length (s) | 120 | 20 | 600 | 10 |\n| `gameSpeed` | Game speed | 1 | .25 | 2 | .05 |\n\n### 14.3 CHEATS (toggles)\n\nGod mode (my team cannot be tackled), infinite turbo, hype always full, always ON FIRE, ref blind (sees nothing), ref\nstrict (sees everything), CPU frozen, no clock, big heads (`headR` × 1.8 on every rig).\n\n### 14.4 EVENTS (one press each; the same `game.debug.force(name)` the attract director and tests use)\n\nScore a try (home / away), give P1 the ball, ball to the opponents' 5 m line, knock-on → scrum, touch → lineout, foul →\npenalty (then the choice), yellow card, kick-off, 22 drop-out, a ruck now, monster hit on the carrier, spawn each\npower-up, trigger the venue's hazard (and each other venue's), ON FIRE for P1's player, Thunder Run, Hurricane Hit,\nreplay the last 5 s, the hooter, skip to halftime, skip to full time, sudden death, set score (+5 / -5 each team),\nset clock (-30 s).\n\n### 14.5 VIEW (debug overlays) and JUMP\n\nVIEW: AI shape slots and roles, the referee's cone (always), offside lines (always), tackle and fend ranges, pass lanes\nand legal receivers, rig skeletons (`rig.debug`), the engine's frame-rate readout, slow motion (0.25x), frame step (□\nadvances one step while FROZEN), state readout (phase, timers, possession). JUMP: any cup round's match or cutscene, any\ndrill, the victory, the third half, the credits, a session-style match (halves 75 s, no replays).\n\n---\n\n## 15. Multiplayer\n\n### 15.1 On its own: local play (1-4 on one machine)\n\n- `game.allowJoin({ max: 4, onJoin })` on the join screen: Start on a free pad, or 2 for the keyboard's right half\n  (patch 2's layouts). Each seat picks HOME, AWAY or CPU; in the cup everyone is on the Mudlarks (co-op).\n- One shared screen (the broadcast camera follows the ball; nobody needs their own view). Each seat has its colour\n  (`E.PLAYER_COLORS`), ring, chevron, HUD card and its own glyphs (its own device).\n- Co-op (all humans on one team vs CPU) and versus (any split, CPU filling the rest). Each human controls one player at\n  a time (§7.2). The pause says who paused (engine).\n\n### 15.2 AI Hub sessions (the protocol at lib/hub/README.md)\n\n**Mode: `shared-world`, 2-4 players.** One match, the host's copy runs it (all 14 players' rules, contact, AI, ball,\nreferee); every other copy sends what its player does and draws what the host sends. It is the most fun mode for a team\nsport and the architecture (§4.1) is built for it. **Fallback:** if the 4-player lossy harness run still fails at the\nend of M8, switch the declared mode to `parallel` (every player plays the same seeded 3-minute match against the CPU;\n`progress` and `result({ score: points for - points against + 100 })`), which the same architecture supports with no\nnetwork code; record the decision in the About screen.\n\n**Tags in `<head>`** (after the session client):\n\n```html\n<meta name=\"hub:session\" content=\"1\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-players\" content=\"2-4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-mode\" content=\"shared-world\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-minutes\" content=\"4\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-goal\" content=\"Score more points than the other team\">\n<meta name=\"hub:session-howto\" content=\"Stick/WASD run · L1/R1 or Q/E pass · ✕/Space tackle or step · ○/C kick\">\n<meta name=\"hub:type\" content=\"Game\">\n<meta name=\"hub:players\" content=\"1-4\">\n```\n\n**Flow:**\n1. At load, `AIHub.active` (true from the first line) builds the game with session options: `pausable: false` on the\n   match, `autoPause: false`, `corner: false`, `fullscreen: false` (the hub owns full screen), `menu: { quit: false,\n   items: [] }`, no title, no attract, no saves, no records, no dev mode, no replays, no cutscenes, no pause.\n2. `const info = await AIHub.connect({ mode: 'shared-world', seats: [2, 4], card: true })`; `null` → the normal game\n   (title).\n3. Setup from `AIHub.rng('setup')` (the same on every copy, no messages needed): the home side is the Mudlarks, the away\n   side one of the four rivals, the venue one of four, the weather the venue's. Seats: even ids HOME, odd AWAY (2\n   players 1v1, 3 players 2v1, 4 players 2v2); CPU fills the rest. Halves: `min(75, (limitMs / 1000 - 40) / 2.6)` s\n   of running clock (75 when `limitMs` is 0); no sudden death (a draw is a tie).\n4. Build the match (boot steps), then show **our card** (scene `card`) and call `AIHub.ready()` once, with no key press.\n   The card: the venue at dusk, both teams lined up, each human's player in front with the hub name in the seat colour\n   (`#e8443a`, `#3b82f6`, `#22c55e`, `#f5b700`) and a READY tick from the `card` event's `ready`; \"HOW TO WIN: score more\n   points than the other team\"; this player's controls as badges for its device (five lines at most); the countdown\n   from `card.left` (\"STARTS IN 12\"), \"PAUSED: players asked for more time\" while `paused`; the title music. Enter or ✕\n   (button 0) or a tap on READY → `AIHub.answer('ready')`; △ (button 3), M, or a tap on MORE TIME →\n   `AIHub.answer('more')`. Nothing on the card draws from `Math.random` (use `AIHub.rng('card')`).\n5. `start` → a 3-2-1 countdown while `AIHub.time() < 0` (big numbers over the kick-off), the whistle at 0, play.\n6. Full time (host): `AIHub.finish({ standings, scores, stats, awards })`: standings `[[winning side's ids], [losing\n   side's ids]]` or `[[everyone]]` for a draw; scores `{ id: their side's points }`; stats per id (tries, tackles, big\n   hits, metres); awards (Player of the Match, Biggest Hit). A last reliable snapshot `{ over: 1 }` first.\n7. `time-up` → the host finishes at once with the current score (within `graceMs`).\n8. `leave` → that seat's player goes back to the CPU; if a side has no humans left the match still runs to full time.\n9. `end` → stop simulation, input and sound (`audio.stopMusic`, mute), show \"FULL TIME\" under the hub's results.\n10. `AIHub.server` (a host with no player): run the same host code with no local seat; `draw` returns at once.\n\n**Messages** (JSON; at most 20 a second leave a copy, bursts to 40; unreliable ones under about 1,200 bytes):\n\n| Message | Direction | Reliable | Rate | Contents |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `i` (intent) | client → host | no | 15/s | `{ t: 'i', s: seq, at: AIHub.time(), m: [mx, my] (×100 ints), h: held bits, p: [[action, at, id], ...] (presses of the last 1 s, deduplicated by id), pos: [x, y] when its player is free-running }` |\n| `s` (snapshot) | host → all | no | 12/s | `{ t: 's', at, ph: phase code, c: clock×10, sc: [h, a], hy: [h, a], b: [x, y, z, state, holder], r: [x, y, f, down], P: [[id, x, y, z, f×100, st, ctl, anim...], ...14] }`, ints only, anim fields only when not default |\n| `e` (events) | host → all | yes | ≤ 5/s, bundled every 200 ms | `{ t: 'e', l: [[id, name, data], ...] }`: scores, phase changes, set-piece setups (with their parameters and timestamps), cards, banners, power-ups, hazards, commentary keys, `over` |\n| `ctl` | host → one | yes | on change | which on-field player the seat now drives |\n\n- **Client-side feel:** like the hub's arena example, each copy moves its own controlled player at once from its own\n  stick while that player is free-running (`idle/run/carry/charge`) and reports `pos`; the host takes that position\n  (clamped to what the player could have run since the last report, +20 u) and decides everything else: contact, the\n  ball, the laws. When the host puts the player into any other state (tackled, ruck, scrum...), the client follows the\n  host's state and eases its drawn position to the snapshot's.\n- **Everyone else on a client** is drawn from snapshots interpolated 120 ms behind (positions lerp, facing by the short\n  arc, discrete states switch at snapshot boundaries); the same presentation code as live play draws them (rigs from\n  the `anim` record, §11.1). Events trigger the same commentary, sound, banners and particles locally. No snapshot for\n  1 s: a small \"WAITING FOR THE HOST...\" note.\n- **Timing mini-games online:** the host stamps each set piece's key moment (scrum SET, lineout throw arrival, kick-off\n  catch) with `AIHub.time()` in its event; clients show the cue against their own `AIHub.time()` and send their press\n  with its `at`; the host judges the press by `|at - T|` (the hub keeps clocks within about 15 ms).\n- **Snapshot budget:** 99% of snapshots ≤ 1,100 bytes (tested, §19.3); if a build exceeds it, send players 0-6 and 7-13\n  in alternate snapshots.\n- **Rules in a session:** no network but the hub; no chat, emotes or custom names (names from the hub, never inserted\n  as HTML: the pixel font draws them, accents stripped with `normalize('NFD')` and any other character it lacks shown\n  as a dot); no `alert`, `confirm`, `prompt`, `window.open` or navigation; no title, restart\n  or quit; Options is ignored while Create is held; no hit-stop (`game.freeze` would stall the host's sends: hits use\n  `particles.impact`, sparks, shake and a sound instead, and each copy may hold its drawn frame for 60 ms); nothing\n  outside a session changes.\n\n---\n\n## 16. Settings and accessibility (own settings via `menu.settings`)\n\n| Key | Group | Label | Options (default first) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `glyphs` (override) | Display | Controller buttons | PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ · Xbox A B X Y |\n| `uiScale` | Display | TV text size | Large · Normal (Large raises HUD prompts to scale 2 and the shell CSS above) |\n| `camera` | Display | Camera | Broadcast · High · Overhead (the views of §5.3; R3 and V cycle them in play) |\n| `difficulty` | Game | Difficulty | Pro · Rookie · Legend |\n| `halves` | Game | Half length | 2 min · 3 · 4 · 5 |\n| `suddenDeath` | Game | Sudden death in Exhibition | On · Off |\n| `autoSwitch` | Game | Auto-switch | Assisted · Full · Manual |\n| `passAssist` | Game | Pass assist | On · Off |\n| `turboMode` | Game | Turbo | Hold · Toggle |\n| `commentary` | Game | Commentary | On · Text only · Off |\n| `replays` | Game | Replays | Big moments · All tries · Off |\n| `refCone` | Game | Show the ref's view | On · Off |\n| `bigHeads` | Game | Big heads (after the cup) | Off · On |\n| `crowd` | Sound | Crowd volume | slider .8 |\n\nThe engine's standard settings stay (volume, music, effects, reduce motion, shake, flashes, pixel scaling, filter,\nframe rate) and are honoured: shake, flash and zoom punches go through `game.shake` / `game.flash` / tweens that check\n`game.reduceMotion`. Information is never in colour alone: teams have crests and three-letter names, seats have labels,\nlines have words.\n\n---\n\n## 17. Build order and milestones\n\nEach milestone ends with its tests passing (`node test/run.mjs`) and, where it says so, screenshots looked at. Do the\nriskiest work first (M1-M2.5).\n\n| M | Build | Exit criteria |\n|---|---|---|\n| **M0 Scaffold** | the dev folder; copy the engine, tools and `package.json` (`npm install`; Playwright's Chromium is already cached on this machine at `~/.cache/ms-playwright`, else `npx playwright install chromium`); fetch `session.js`, `harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` (`curl -fsSO https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/main/lib/hub/<file>`); `build.mjs`, `page.html` with the hub tags; the vm loader and `test/run.mjs`; a placeholder-free stub (a title scene with the logo). Practical notes: `/tmp` on this machine was full during planning, so keep scratch files in the dev folder; take the engine's `dist/handoff` with `git clone --depth 1` of my-3d2dge (about 150 MB) and the hub files with `curl` (the ai-hub repository is over 5 GB: do not clone it) | `node test/run.mjs` prints `N passed, 0 failed`; the built HTML passes `tools/check.mjs`; a test asserts the session client is the first script in `<head>` and unmodified |\n| **M1 Pitch, players, ball (spikes)** | geometry, TileMap, `floorTex` lines, posts, stands, boards; the `broadcast` view; 14 dressed Humanoids in formation; one human-controlled player with turbo; the carry pose; the loose oval ball, pick-up; the camera; the radar. **Spikes:** a superman dive (`down: 1` at height) and the tackle as a paired `E.Action`; `r.pick` on a teammate; 14 rigs + crowd on screen | ball physics tests; screenshots of the pitch in three views at 1280x720 and 1920x1080 look like a broadcast; the dive and tackle filmstrips read |\n| **M2 Core loop** | passing (L1/R1, legal receivers, catches, knock-ons), the tackle → simple ruck → recycle, tries and grounding, conversions (simple), kick-off (simple), the clock, halves, hooter, full time; attack and defence shapes; auto-switch; `MatchState` and intents in place | a CPU v CPU match plays to full time with scores and no stalls (watchdog never fires); a scripted human scores a try |\n| **M2.5 Net spike** | `MatchState` snapshot encoding; the fake hub (in-process, latency/jitter/loss); host + one client in Node: the client's intents move its player, its snapshots draw | the loopback test passes; snapshot size test passes |\n| **M3 Laws and set pieces** | forward pass, knock-on → scrum; touch → lineout; set kicks with the kicker camera (yaw spike); penalties and the choice; free kicks; drop goals; 22 drop-outs; the referee (runs, sees, calls, signals); offside lines; advantage; cards and the sin bin; penalty try; \"use it\" | law and set-piece tests; CPU v CPU matches visit every set piece (coverage) |\n| **M4 Arcade layer** | sidestep, spin, fend, dummy, offload, interceptions; big and monster hits; dive and gang tackles; cheap shots; hype and supers; ON FIRE; power-ups; the four hazards and weather; ref down; instant replay | a test per system; across 20 seeded CPU matches with forcing, every event fires |\n| **M5 Presentation** | the full HUD with badges; commentary and babble (patch 4); music and SFX; crowd; lights; effects; every animation in §11.2 polished; venue dressing (boards, floodlights, jumbotron, skylines); patches 1 and 3 | `tools/check.mjs` look notes acted on; screenshots of each venue, set piece and HUD state reviewed; filmstrips of every move reviewed |\n| **M6 Modes and content** | title, menu, cup hub, the story scenes and sets, halftime talks, results, victory, third half, credits; Exhibition setup; the join screen and local multiplayer (patch 2); the Training Ground's nine drills with SHOW ME; records and initials; About & Credits; saves and CONTINUE; unlocks | the scripted full playthrough passes (§19.4); save and continue tests pass |\n| **M7 Platform** | the attract mode and its director; touch-only mode; dev mode (panel, tuning, cheats, events, view, jump, hotkeys); own settings and the glyph override; the TV CSS for the shell; device-following prompts everywhere | attract, dev, glyph and touch-only tests pass |\n| **M8 AI Hub** | session options; connect; our card; host, client and server paths; intents, snapshots and events; finish, time-up, leave, end | the loopback suite (4 players, 150 ms ± 50, 5% loss; host leave; server) passes; the real harness passes in all four configurations (§19.6); standalone still plays |\n| **M9 Balance, polish, ship** | tune with the dev panel to the targets in §19.7; difficulty through the cup; a bug bash; final About text and exit interview | every test passes; the final HTML checked in Chromium (no errors); the About screen's text is real; `exit-interview.json` parses |\n\n---\n\n## 18. Risks and how to de-risk them\n\n| Risk | Why it is risky | De-risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| The AI plays like bees round a jam pot | 14 players chasing the ball look nothing like rugby | role slots and \"only two chase\" (§7.19) from M2; metric tests (defensive line spread ≥ 300 u across 6 players, support depth behind the carrier, no more than 3 players within 40 u of the ball in open play 90% of the time) |\n| Phases that never end | a ruck with nobody committed, a ball stuck under a pile, a set piece waiting forever | a watchdog on every phase (§7.1); tests fail on any watchdog; the attract director doubles as a soak test |\n| Tackles, dives and scrums look wrong | rigs are procedural; flat dives and binds are new | M1 spikes; paired `E.ACTION`s; filmstrip reviews; fallbacks: the engine's `down` and `getUp`, `crouch` and `lean` alone |\n| Clutter at 427x240 with 15 rigs and a crowd | the player loses the ball and himself | contrasting kits (luminance rule), seat rings, x-ray outlines, the ball carrier outline, zoom .9, the radar, off-screen arrows; screenshot review in M1 and M5 |\n| The backwards-pass rule frustrates | new players pass forward and get whistled | receivers are chosen behind by default; the L1/R1 badges show only legal receivers; the mouse hover shows red for forward; drill 2 teaches it |\n| Online shared world | lag, loss, a contact sport, timing mini-games | the state/presentation split from M2; the M2.5 spike; client-moved own player; time-stamped presses; ints in snapshots; the loopback suite; no replays or cutscenes online; fallback to `parallel` (§15.2) |\n| Hub compliance traps | the engine pauses, quits, goes full screen and stores by default | the session option list (§15.2); harness runs early in M8 (a minimal card can be tried at M2.5) |\n| Glyphs and fonts | the font lacks PS symbols; pads report odd names | patch 1; badges; label tests with `input.glyphs` both ways |\n| Determinism and flaky tests | randomness and timing | `TT.rng` only in the simulation; fixed seeds in tests; no wall clock in the simulation |\n| The single-file bundle | script order, the session client first, `</script>` in strings | `build.mjs` checks; a test parses the final HTML; `tools/check.mjs` on the final file |\n| Slow headless tests | rigs cost time | `TT.cfg.present = false` headless |\n| Scope | a lot of content | the order above makes the game whole by M4; content after; every system stays even if story lines get shorter |\n| Touch-only false positives | touch laptops | require no fine pointer; leave the mode on any key, mouse or pad |\n\n---\n\n## 19. Testing\n\n### 19.1 One command\n\n`node test/run.mjs` (from the dev folder) rebuilds, runs every headless suite, and prints **only failures** (`FAIL\nsuite › case: message`) and **one line**: `N passed, M failed (S suites, T s)`; exit code 1 on any failure. `--only\n<suite>` reruns one suite (rerun a suite only after changing the code it covers). `--browser` adds the Playwright\nchecks, `--hub` the AI Hub harness runs (both print the same way). Tests are scripts that play the game and check its\nstate through `game.advance` and `game.debug`; screenshots only for what a script cannot check; no subagents or\nworkflows to debate, review, judge or audit (TASK.md).\n\n### 19.2 The harness\n\n`test/lib/load.mjs` creates a `vm` context with stand-ins (`window` = the context, `addEventListener`,\n`removeEventListener`, `location { hash, search }`, `matchMedia(() => ({ matches: false }))`, `navigator {\ngetGamepads: () => [] }`, `performance`, a quiet `console`), runs `engine/my-3d2dge.js` then `build/game.js`, and\nreturns `{ game, TT, E }` (no DOM, so the engine is headless). Helpers: `snap()`, `advance(s)`, `tap(action, seat)`,\n`hold(action, on, seat)`, `feed(pointer events)`, `untilPhase(name, maxS)`, `playMatch(opts)` (CPU v CPU or autopilot),\n`fakeHub(n, { latency, jitter, loss, server, hostLeave })` (several contexts wired through an in-process network with\n`AIHub` stand-ins that implement `connect`, `ready`, `answer`, `time`, `on`, `send`, `finish`, `rng`, `active`).\n\n### 19.3 Suites (what each checks)\n\n| Suite | Checks |\n|---|---|\n| `boot` | boots headless with no errors; every scene enters and exits cleanly; `game.debug.snapshot()` fields present; the final HTML has the session client first, unmodified, and every hub tag |\n| `ball` | pass arcs land within 4 u of the lead point; punts land within 5% of their predicted spot without wind; the oval bounce stays within its angle and height bounds and is the same for the same seed; touch at the right x; dead-ball → drop-out |\n| `laws` | backward and flat passes legal; forward (relative to the passer) whistled; a sprinting lateral pass legal; knock-on → scrum to the other team; tries only when grounded in the in-goal; 5/2/3/3/7 scoring; conversion geometry (in line with the grounding); the hooter waits for a stoppage; sudden death ends on the first score |\n| `contact` | tackle success over 400 seeded tries within ±5% of the formula; big hits launch beyond 30 u; the fend window beats tackles at the expected rate; a sidestep makes tackles whiff; BIG UNIT needs two tacklers; a cheap shot seen (ref facing) is a penalty and unseen (ref turned away) is not |\n| `breakdown` | ruck results match §7.8's worked example (two attackers against one: a human at 0/s loses to a CPU at 9/s, at 12/s beats a CPU at 6/s); jackal swing; hands in the ruck; \"use it\" → scrum; offside flagged only when seen |\n| `setpieces` | scrum timing windows (perfect, good, early engagement → free kick); push outcomes; pushover try; lineout calls, steals and not straight; kick-off ten metres; shot clock; penalty choices each lead to the right restart |\n| `arcade` | hype gains per event; each super starts, works and ends; ON FIRE starts on the second try and ends on the opponents' score; each power-up spawns, is taken and expires; each hazard warns then strikes and drops a carrier's ball; ref down silences fouls for 8 s |\n| `ai` | 30 seeded CPU v CPU matches (30 s halves): no watchdog, no errors, scores 0-80 a side, tries per match 2-14 on average 4-9, both teams pass, tackle, kick, ruck and scrum; shape metrics (§18); the AI never stands offside at a set piece |\n| `control` | auto-switch modes; L1/R1 and ○ switching; mouse click on a teammate passes or switches; a seat never takes a player held by another seat |\n| `input` | `label()` gives ✕ ○ □ △ by default on a pad, A B X Y after `settings.set('glyphs', 'xbox')`, keys on the keyboard; prompts change with `lastDevice`; half-keyboard layouts (patch 2) map as §6.3; the font draws ✕ ○ □ △ (patch 1: no `?` glyph used) |\n| `replay` | the ring buffer holds 5 s; a replay plays and the match resumes exactly where it was |\n| `proof` | the kit-style proof: a scripted 600-step match from seed 7 gives the same hash twice |\n| `drills` | each drill completes with its `auto` script; each one's failure path resets |\n| `attract` | 10 s of idle on the title starts it; a 4-minute attract loop fires every showcase step once; a key, a pad button and a mouse move each return to the title without picking anything; touch-only mode shows the message and still runs it |\n| `dev` | DEV MODE from the pause menu opens the panel; each slider changes `TUNE.v` and the simulation (run speed → measured speed); reset restores every default; each cheat and event works |\n| `saves` | the cup autosaves; CONTINUE resumes at the right round; records and unlocks persist; a bad save falls back |\n| `local` | four seats join (pads and the right keyboard half); 2v2 and co-op matches run with each seat driving its own player |\n| `net` | loopback: 2 players clean; 4 players at 150 ± 50 ms and 5% loss for 60 s of play; clients' players move from their intents; snapshots ≤ 1,100 bytes 99% of the time and under 12/s; events arrive once; `finish` once with valid standings at full time; `time-up` finishes within grace; a leaver's player goes CPU; host leave ends cleanly; server mode runs with no local seat; card answers ready on Enter; no `Math.random` use on the card |\n\n### 19.4 The scripted playthrough of the whole game\n\n`playthrough.test.mjs`: title → PRESS START → menu → MEGA CUP → prologue (pages turned) → TRAIN → each of the nine\ndrills by its `auto` script → pre-match scene → round one played honestly by the autopilot (the human seat driven by the\nCPU brain through the intent interface, like the sports kit's `bot()`; halves 30 s) until won (retry with CONTINUE if\nlost; the test fails after three losses) → results → cup hub (autosave checked) → quarter-final, semi-final and grand\nfinal (halves 30 s; god mode allowed for the final only, so the boss's gang-tackle rule is still exercised by the CPU)\n→ victory → third half → credits → title. It asserts the scene order, the saves, the unlocks, and no errors or\nwatchdogs anywhere.\n\n### 19.5 In a browser (what scripts cannot check)\n\n- `node tools/check.mjs <final html>` (and `#scene` deep links: `#match`, `#cup`, `#training`, `#attract`): no errors,\n  look notes acted on; `--size 1920x1080` and `--size 390x844@3` (touch-only screen).\n- Screenshots to look at (once per milestone that changes them): each venue in each view; kick-off, ruck, scrum,\n  lineout, set kick, penalty choice; a monster hit; a replay; the HUD with 1 and 4 seats; each menu screen; the dev\n  panel; the hub card; results; credits.\n- Filmstrips (`tools/filmstrip.mjs --seed 1`) of every move in §11.2.\n- A pad by hand if one is available (else `input.press('Pad0')` scripted in Playwright).\n\n### 19.6 The AI Hub harness\n\nServe the dev folder's `hub/` (holding the harness files and a copy of the built HTML) with `python3 -m http.server 0\n--bind 127.0.0.1` (note the port it prints and its PID), fetch the copied HTML from that port and check its `<title>`\nis \"Thugby Turbo\" (another agent's server may hold a port: never trust a page you have not checked), then with\nPlaywright open `harness.html?game=<html>&players=2&auto=1`; `&players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1`;\nthe same with `&scenario=host-leave`; and `&players=2&scenario=server`. Wait for `document.body.dataset.complete ===\n'true'`; pass when `window.__hubCheck.pass` is true; print failing checks only. Stop only the server you started, by its\nPID. Then open the HTML on its own to confirm it still plays outside a session.\n\n### 19.7 Balance targets (tuned with the dev panel, measured by the `ai` suite and by hand)\n\nA Pro v Pro CPU match: 4-9 tries, 60% of conversions, 1-3 monster hits, 2-4 scrums, 2-4 lineouts, a card every two or\nthree matches, a power-up taken every 40 s. A new human on Pro wins round one about four times in five and loses the\nfinal at the first try about half the time. Turbo runs out in about one long break. A big hit should feel like an event,\nnot every tackle.\n\n---\n\n## 20. About & Credits screen and the exit interview\n\n- **About & Credits** (`about` scene, five pages at scale 1-2, L1/R1 or left/right, ✕ closes):\n  1. THUGBY TURBO: made with the my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (`E.versionLabel()`), the AI Hub session client 1.4.0; by\n     the model that built it; the title's RNG pick; \"an original game inspired by rugby; every team, person and place\n     is made up\".\n  2. SOURCE INFORMATION: Date 2026-11-03 17:48:28 · Source: rugby (over-the-top arcade style) · Plan: PLAN.md · Genre\n     and perspective · Tech · Art direction · Animation · Audio · Online · Testing · Research · Environment · Model ·\n     Usage (estimates only if available; \"unknown\" otherwise).\n  3. TESTING FEEDBACK (the four answers). 4. ENGINE FEEDBACK (the six answers, including the patches and why). 5. PROMPT\n     FEEDBACK (the four answers).\n  The builder writes these from the real build at the end (M9); \"unknown\" for anything that can't be confirmed. The\n  same text goes in the final chat reply.\n- **`exit-interview.json`** in this folder, beside the HTML, in exactly TASK.md's shape; suggested `aiHub` values:\n  name \"THUGBY TURBO\", tagline \"Big hits, bigger hair, seven a side\", description \"Over-the-top arcade rugby sevens:\n  turbo runs, monster hits, a referee who only sees half of it, and a pub team's run at the Mega Cup.\", type \"Game\",\n  genre \"Sports\", source \"Rugby\", series \"\", players \"1–4\", controls \"Stick or WASD run, L1/R1 or Q/E pass, ✕ or Space\n  tackle and sidestep, ○ or C kick, R2 or Shift turbo, △ or R super\", tags `[\"sports\", \"arcade\", \"comedy\", \"pixel art\",\n  \"versus\", \"co-op\"]`. Check it parses (`node -e \"JSON.parse(require('fs').readFileSync('exit-interview.json','utf8'))\"`).\n\n---\n\n## 21. Definition of done\n\n- [ ] `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174828.html` in this folder plays from `file://` with no errors;\n      the dev subfolder holds the sources, tests and tools.\n- [ ] Every system in §7 works in play and is shown by the cup, the drills or the attract mode; nothing is stubbed; no\n      TODO or placeholder text anywhere (grep the sources for TODO, FIXME, TBD, lorem, placeholder).\n- [ ] Mega Cup (prologue, training, four matches, victory, third half, credits), Exhibition (any teams, venues,\n      weather, 1-4 players), Training Ground, Records, Settings, About & Credits all reachable and complete.\n- [ ] Controller-first 10-foot UI; PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox in Settings; keyboard and mouse native; prompts\n      follow the last device; touch-only screen with Settings; attract mode after 10 s, any input exits.\n- [ ] Dev mode from the pause menu: live sliders for §14.2, reset to defaults, cheats, events, overlays, jumps.\n- [ ] All audio in code; babble voices for every speaker; music per scene with layers.\n- [ ] Local multiplayer 1-4; AI Hub shared-world passes the harness in all four configurations; outside a session the\n      game is unaffected.\n- [ ] `node test/run.mjs` passes, including the scripted playthrough; browser checks and screenshots reviewed.\n- [ ] Engine patches listed in `engine/PATCHES.md` and in Engine Feedback.\n- [ ] About & Credits pages and the chat reply carry Source Information, Testing, Engine and Prompt Feedback;\n      `exit-interview.json` parses.\n","numLines":360,"startLine":1320,"totalLines":1679}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Y164xrEQzV7JVT88NSvMsX","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
