{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi5QjtHoXhCW5LWjbip6","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I've built **Thugby Turbo**, an over-the-top arcade rugby sevens game. It's one self-contained file in this folder: `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html` (1.6 MB, no downloads needed), with `exit-interview.json` beside it. All 168 headless checks pass, Chromium showed no errors on any screen I opened, and the real AI Hub harness passes in all four of its configurations.\n\n**What's in it:** the story campaign (four cup matches with cutscenes, victory and credits), Exhibition for 1–4 players on one machine, nine training drills with a \"show me\" demo, high-score tables, Settings (PlayStation buttons by default, Xbox as an option) and the About & Credits screen. Matches include passing (backwards only), tackles, big hits that launch players, rucks, scrums, lineouts, penalties, conversions, drop goals, cards and a sin bin, power-ups, a hazard at each stadium, supers, \"on fire\", instant replays and live commentary. The attract demo starts after 10 seconds idle, and dev mode is in the pause menu.\n\n**Where I scaled the plan back:**\n- **Kicker camera:** conversions use the plan's own fallback, an aim gauge showing the posts from behind, not a second camera.\n- **Online play:** a client's own player isn't moved locally ahead of the host. It shows the host's position, so there's a small delay under lag.\n- **Mega Cup save:** it uses simple saved data rather than the engine's save slots.\n- **10-foot menus:** the game draws its own large menus instead of patching the engine's.\n\nMudlarks win most CPU-vs-CPU matches; they're just the strongest team, not a side bias. The dev files (sources, tests, tools, `engine/PATCHES.md`, `docs/DATA.md`) are in the `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-…/` subfolder.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, training drills and multiplayer on one machine or online. A TV broadcast camera (the pitch left to right, seen from the stand at 40°), with High and Overhead options; set kicks use a behind-the-posts aim gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (canvas 2D and Web Audio, 3 small patches); AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, unmodified; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 are used for tests only. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel style. Each player is a dressed 3D character in their team kit, on a striped pitch with stands and a crowd in team colours. The oval ball, posts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and hazards are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's character rig, driven from match state, plus moves made for this game: diving tackles, flips when launched, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stunned stars, and a skeleton flash when hit by lightning.\n- **Audio:** All in code: seven songs (venue themes add drum and hype layers as play heats up), eight jingles, about 30 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2–4 players, with the game's own card; finish, time-up, a player leaving and a host with no player of its own are all handled. Local play for 1–4 on one machine (controllers and two keyboard halves).\n- **Testing:** One command (`node test/run.mjs`) runs 15 suites, 168 checks in about 25 s, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup and a two-copy online test in one process. Playwright screenshots of the key screens; the real AI Hub harness in all four configurations; the engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine handoff package (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md, the sports kit, parts of the engine source) and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files. No other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no token or cost meter available); session time about 1 hour\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 18:\n   - The computer-controlled carriers almost never passed, then passed so much that matches ended 0–0.\n   - Three crashes at startup.\n   - A crash in the camera code.\n   - Kicks launched from where the ball used to be after a player was moved.\n   - Defenders caught drop goals straight off the boot.\n   - Penalty tries checked the wrong player.\n   - The final's giant boss was launched instead of needing two tacklers.\n   - Winning a scrum by collapsing it didn't count in the scrum drill.\n   - An online human kicker could stall the kick-off.\n   - Five layout bugs found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four: the three startup crashes and the camera crash. All are fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup (menus, story, training, four autopiloted matches, initials, credits), together with the drill scripts that prove each of the nine drills can be completed.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed gives the same match. It never failed, because the simulation was deterministic from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Running the game without a browser in exact fixed steps. Whole matches, the full cup and an online test with two copies run in seconds, and the same code draws in the browser.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** A few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key, which one of my tests didn't expect.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately rather than being queued.\n   - I had to find the right buffer to draw the skyline on before the pitch.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** The match simulation (rugby laws, set pieces, advantage, cards), the team AI, and one input layer that lets humans, the AI, tests, the attract demo and online clients all drive the same players.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 buffer. There is no built-in big menu or button-badge widget.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font; they drew as \"?\".\n   - Made the two-players-on-one-keyboard key layout configurable per action.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box so each speaker can have a babble voice.\n6. **One thing I would change:** Make controller-glyph badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** It is concrete about what \"done\" means (attract mode, dev sliders, buttons that follow the last device, the touch-only screen, AI Hub), and the detailed plan meant building rather than guessing.\n2. **What was confusing:** The plan's file names (max effort, an earlier date) don't match TASK.md's. STAGE.md settles it (TASK.md wins), but it took a moment. The plan's very large scope also pulls against a \"feature-complete demo\" at medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Making the sport's AI look like rugby while keeping every system reachable in play, in the drills and in the attract demo, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What I would change:** Fill the plan's names in from TASK.md's values, and mark which plan items are optional polish and which are required features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1352,"cache_read_input_tokens":537472,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1352},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"641c7116-2871-4be8-abad-9d1a3c8e6028","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:52:29.424Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi5QjVygACtJGue2WsXs"}
