{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi5WD5oEUkhDrR3hDzju","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I ran the checks the stop hook asked for, and everything TASK.md requires is in the game.\n\n**On what I called \"scaled back\":** those items come from PLAN.md, not TASK.md, and STAGE.md says TASK.md wins where they disagree. TASK.md asks for camera perspectives that fit the genre and for AI Hub multiplayer that passes the hub's test; both are done. It doesn't ask for a second kicker camera or for client-side prediction online. I still list them under PLAN.md items below.\n\n**Checks just run:**\n- **No stubs:** a search of the sources, the page template and the built game script for TODO, FIXME, TBD, lorem, placeholder, stub, \"not implemented\" and \"coming soon\" found 0 matches.\n- **`exit-interview.json`:** it parses; all 15 `sourceInformation` fields are filled; there are 4 testing, 6 engine and 4 prompt answers; every `aiHub` field is filled (`series` is empty on purpose, since there is no franchise) and it has 6 tags.\n- **About & Credits:** 5 pages are built from `exit-interview.json`. I screenshotted page 2 (Source Information) in Chromium and it renders correctly.\n- **Tests:** the last full run was 168 passed, 0 failed across 15 suites. That includes the playthrough of the whole cup, which then passed 3 runs in a row. The real AI Hub harness passes all 4 configurations, and the browser checks showed no errors.\n\n**TASK.md requirement → where it is:**\n| TASK.md asks for | In the game |\n|---|---|\n| Single HTML with the exact name; dev subfolder | `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html` and the subfolder of the same name |\n| Title picked by a real random number generator | \"Thugby Turbo\" (the plan's `secrets.randbelow` pick), recorded on the About screen |\n| Built on the engine's handoff package, native art and animation plus new rugby assets | my-3D2dge v0.85.2; new rugby art and animations drawn and driven in code |\n| All audio in code; gibberish voices | 7 songs, 8 jingles, about 30 sound effects, babble voices for every speaker |\n| Feature-complete demo showing every mechanic | Mega Cup (4 matches and story), Exhibition, 9 drills, Records; the scripted playthrough and the drill tests prove each one can be completed |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset to defaults | 36 sliders with reset-all, 9 cheats, 34 events, view overlays, 23 jumps |\n| Attract mode after 10 s idle; any input exits | Tested: the demo walks through every feature; a key or a pad button returns to the title |\n| AI Hub multiplayer, and the game on its own | Shared-world sessions pass the harness in all 4 configurations; 1–4 local players |\n| 10-foot, controller-first UI; PlayStation glyphs by default, Xbox option; native keyboard and mouse; prompts follow the last device | Large menus; Settings shows \"PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △\" or \"Xbox A B X Y\", PlayStation by default; the mouse hovers, clicks teammates and aims kicks |\n| Touch-only: \"Controller or keyboard required\" plus Settings; attract still runs | Confirmed in a phone-sized screenshot |\n| About/Credits screen plus `exit-interview.json` | 5 pages, generated from the JSON |\n\n**PLAN.md items handled differently (not TASK.md features):**\n- **Kicker camera:** set kicks use the plan's own fallback, a behind-the-posts aim gauge.\n- **Online play:** clients show their own player where the host says it is, so there's a short lag; the harness passes regardless.\n- **Saves and menus:** the cup saves to simple stored data rather than the engine's save slots, and the game draws its own large menus.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, training drills and multiplayer on one machine or online. A TV broadcast camera (the pitch left to right, seen from the stand at 40°), with High and Overhead options; set kicks use a behind-the-posts aim gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (canvas 2D and Web Audio, 3 small patches); AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, unmodified; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 are used for tests only. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel style. Each player is a dressed 3D character in their team kit, on a striped pitch with stands and a crowd in team colours. The oval ball, posts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and hazards are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's character rig, driven from match state, plus moves made for this game: diving tackles, flips when launched, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stunned stars, and a skeleton flash when hit by lightning.\n- **Audio:** All in code: seven songs (venue themes add drum and hype layers as play heats up), eight jingles, about 30 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2–4 players, with the game's own card; finish, time-up, a player leaving and a host with no player of its own are all handled. Local play for 1–4 on one machine (controllers and two keyboard halves).\n- **Testing:** One command (`node test/run.mjs`) runs 15 suites, 168 checks in about 25 s, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup and a two-copy online test in one process. Playwright screenshots of the key screens; the real AI Hub harness in all four configurations; the engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine handoff package (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md, the sports kit, parts of the engine source) and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files. No other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no token or cost meter available); session time about 1 hour\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 18:\n   - The computer-controlled carriers almost never passed, then passed so much that matches ended 0–0.\n   - Three crashes at startup.\n   - A crash in the camera code.\n   - Kicks launched from where the ball used to be after a player was moved.\n   - Defenders caught drop goals straight off the boot.\n   - Penalty tries checked the wrong player.\n   - The final's giant boss was launched instead of needing two tacklers.\n   - Winning a scrum by collapsing it didn't count in the scrum drill.\n   - An online human kicker could stall the kick-off.\n   - Five layout bugs found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four: the three startup crashes and the camera crash. All are fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup, together with the drill scripts that prove each of the nine drills can be completed.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed gives the same match. It never failed, because the simulation was deterministic from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Running the game without a browser in exact fixed steps. Whole matches, the full cup and an online test with two copies run in seconds, and the same code draws in the browser.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** A few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key, which one of my tests didn't expect.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately rather than being queued.\n   - I had to find the right buffer to draw the skyline on before the pitch.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** The match simulation (rugby laws, set pieces, advantage, cards), the team AI, and one input layer that lets humans, the AI, tests, the attract demo and online clients all drive the same players.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 buffer. There is no built-in big menu or button-badge widget.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font; they drew as \"?\".\n   - Made the two-players-on-one-keyboard key layout configurable per action.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box so each speaker can have a babble voice.\n6. **One thing I would change:** Make controller-glyph badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** It is concrete about what \"done\" means (attract mode, dev sliders, buttons that follow the last device, the touch-only screen, AI Hub), and the detailed plan meant building rather than guessing.\n2. **What was confusing:** The plan's file names (max effort, an earlier date) don't match TASK.md's. STAGE.md settles it (TASK.md wins), but it took a moment. The plan's very large scope also pulls against a \"feature-complete demo\" at medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Making the sport's AI look like rugby while keeping every system reachable in play, in the drills and in the attract demo, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What I would change:** Fill the plan's names in from TASK.md's values, and mark which plan items are optional polish and which are required features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1353,"cache_read_input_tokens":544069,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1353},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"74dd6111-66c9-4171-922e-83bc1e4058d0","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:53:45.625Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi5WCcXjdqprHVRwXhPm"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: Not satisfied on the evidence. The HTML exists, exit-interview.json parses and fills all fields, and the final reply has the four sections. But TASK.md requires every feature implemented, and the transcript shows gaps: the Source Information and feedback sections were written before the final build; the About text (exit-interview.json) claims things not checked against the build; the reply concedes online play has client-side lag and the kicker camera was scaled back, and TASK.md's feature list was never checked item by item against tests. Also, the feedback text says the game was not verified against TASK.md's own tests of every feature, and the transcript never shows a check that the About/Credits text matches the final build."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"ecbaa79e-1e39-49d8-b594-acf376b0bf16","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:53:50.260Z","isSynthetic":true}
