{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi5kM8NgGfxmdxJo7mWQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I fixed what this round raised:\n- **Online lag:** each online client now moves its own player straight from its own stick and reports the position. The host accepts it within what the player could have run and still decides tackles, the ball and the laws.\n- **About vs build:** a new test checks that the About & Credits text inside the shipped HTML matches `exit-interview.json` answer for answer.\n- **TASK.md coverage:** every requirement is mapped to a passing test in the table below.\n\n`exit-interview.json` and the HTML were rebuilt together at 07:55. The last full run is 172 passed, 0 failed, and the browser checks show no errors. The real AI Hub harness passes all four configurations on the build with the online change. The final rebuild after that only updated the About text, and I didn't re-run the harness on it. A search of the shipped game code for TODO/FIXME/TBD/lorem/placeholder found 0 matches.\n\n| TASK.md asks for | Proven by |\n|---|---|\n| Single HTML with the TASK.md file name, in this folder | It exists (`…-medium-20261104-065619.html`); boot tests read the shipped file |\n| Built on the engine; native art and animation plus rugby assets | All suites run the real engine; browser screenshots of every key screen |\n| All audio in code; gibberish voices | Babble runs per letter in the browser checks; there are no audio files |\n| Every mechanic shown | Rules, contact, set-piece and arcade suites; 9 drill completion tests; playthrough of the whole cup |\n| Dev mode from the pause menu, live sliders, reset | Dev tests: slider changes the measured speed, reset restores defaults, all 34 events and 23 jumps run |\n| Attract mode after 10 s, any input exits | Attract tests: starts at 10 s, every director step fires, a key or a pad button exits |\n| AI Hub multiplayer, and the game on its own | Real harness ×4; loopback net suite (9 checks, including the local move); a 4-player local test |\n| PlayStation default, Xbox option, prompts follow the device, keyboard and mouse native | Input tests (✕/○ by default, A after switching, keys on the keyboard, the two-keyboard layout patch, the font) |\n| Touch-only message plus Settings; attract still runs | Phone-sized screenshot |\n| About/Credits plus `exit-interview.json` | New boot tests: every answer on screen matches the JSON |\n\nThe behind-the-posts aim gauge is PLAN.md's own fallback for set kicks, and TASK.md doesn't require a second camera.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, training drills and multiplayer on one machine or online. A TV broadcast camera (the pitch left to right, seen from the stand at 40°), with High and Overhead options; set kicks use a behind-the-posts aim gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (canvas 2D and Web Audio, 3 small patches); AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, unmodified; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 are used for tests only. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel style. Each player is a dressed 3D character in their team kit, on a striped pitch with stands and a crowd in team colours. The oval ball, posts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and hazards are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's character rig, driven from match state, plus moves made for this game: diving tackles, flips when launched, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stunned stars, and a skeleton flash when hit by lightning.\n- **Audio:** All in code: seven songs (venue themes add drum and hype layers as play heats up), eight jingles, about 30 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2–4 players. The host runs the match; each client moves its own player locally while it's running free and the host accepts that position within limits. Clients draw 12 snapshots a second, with event bundles driving local commentary and effects. The game shows its own card, and finish, time-up, a player leaving and a host with no player of its own are all handled. Local play for 1–4 on one machine (controllers and two keyboard halves).\n- **Testing:** One command (`node test/run.mjs`) runs 15 suites, 172 checks in about 27 s, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup, a proof for each of the nine drills, the attract demo, dev mode and a two-copy online test in one process. Playwright screenshots of the key screens; the real AI Hub harness in all four configurations; the engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine handoff package (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md, the sports kit, parts of the engine source) and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files. No other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no token or cost meter available); session time about 1 hour\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 18:\n   - The computer-controlled carriers almost never passed, then passed so much that matches ended 0–0.\n   - Three crashes at startup.\n   - A crash in the camera code.\n   - Kicks launched from where the ball used to be after a player was moved.\n   - Defenders caught drop goals straight off the boot.\n   - Penalty tries checked the wrong player.\n   - The final's giant boss was launched instead of needing two tacklers.\n   - Winning a scrum by collapsing it didn't count in the scrum drill.\n   - An online human kicker could stall the kick-off.\n   - Five layout bugs found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four: the three startup crashes and the camera crash. All are fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup, together with the drill scripts that prove each of the nine drills can be completed.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed gives the same match. It never failed, because the simulation was deterministic from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Running the game without a browser in exact fixed steps. Whole matches, the full cup and an online test with two copies run in seconds, and the same code draws in the browser.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** A few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key, which one of my tests didn't expect.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately rather than being queued.\n   - I had to find the right buffer to draw the skyline on before the pitch.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** The match simulation (rugby laws, set pieces, advantage, cards), the team AI, and one input layer that lets humans, the AI, tests, the attract demo and online clients all drive the same players.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 buffer. There is no built-in big menu or button-badge widget.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font; they drew as \"?\".\n   - Made the two-players-on-one-keyboard key layout configurable per action.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box so each speaker can have a babble voice.\n6. **One thing I would change:** Make controller-glyph badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** It is concrete about what \"done\" means (attract mode, dev sliders, buttons that follow the last device, the touch-only screen, AI Hub), and the detailed plan meant building rather than guessing.\n2. **What was confusing:** The plan's file names (max effort, an earlier date) don't match TASK.md's. STAGE.md settles it (TASK.md wins), but it took a moment. The plan's very large scope also pulls against a \"feature-complete demo\" at medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Making the sport's AI look like rugby while keeping every system reachable in play, in the drills and in the attract demo, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What I would change:** Fill the plan's names in from TASK.md's values, and mark which plan items are optional polish and which are required features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":997,"cache_read_input_tokens":553348,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":997},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"aba677c3-7bdd-4abc-9dd3-8c6c709e3b8f","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:56:54.747Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi5kLoY4Jwrd2H42e2QB"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The HTML exists in the folder root and exit-interview.json parses with every field filled, and the final reply has all four sections. But the transcript does not show every TASK.md feature verified: the final reply says the final rebuild after the last harness run was not re-tested against the real AI Hub harness, and the feature-to-test table is self-reported rather than checked item by item. The Source Information and feedback text was written before the final build and never re-checked against it (the About-matches-JSON test exists, but the shipped build's About text was not regenerated and re-verified after the last edit). Also the reply's own table conceded the aim gauge replaces a kicker camera, and PLAN.md items were scaled back, which the completion criteria do not explicitly exempt. The stop hook also fired repeatedly, which shows the agent has not yet reached a verified state."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"f6934f13-ffa1-4f70-9c5e-d2b902541e74","timestamp":"2026-11-04T14:56:59.315Z","isSynthetic":true}
