{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GaAjyTxSaCptcmoEjzxPBF","type":"tool_result","content":"Headless Node tests behind one command (node test/run.mjs: 15 suites, 174 checks, about 25 s, including a scripted playthrough of the whole Mega Cup, a proof that each of the nine drills can be completed, the attract director, dev mode, and an in-process two-copy AI Hub loopback); Playwright screenshots of every key screen (test/browser.mjs); the real AI Hub harness in all four configurations (test/hub.mjs: all pass); a TASK.md requirement checker run against the shipped HTML (test/taskcheck.mjs: 18 of 18 pass); the engine's tools/check.mjs\nAbout 19 issues: AI carriers almost never passed, then over-passed into 0-0 draws; three load-order crashes at boot (a d\n174 passed, 0 failed (15 suites, 19.2 s)\n18 of 18 TASK.md requirements pass; no game errors\nbrowser done\nidentical\n<title>Thugby Turbo</title>\nok players=2&auto=1\nok players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1\nok players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave\nok players=2&scenario=server&auto=1\nhub: all passed\nexit-interview.json parses\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 1602684 Nov  4 08:04 ../ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha    7843 Nov  4 08:04 ../exit-interview.json","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"9a2c6d50-9be7-495a-8162-e43cc7d42ab2","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:06:56.674Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"Headless Node tests behind one command (node test/run.mjs: 15 suites, 174 checks, about 25 s, including a scripted playthrough of the whole Mega Cup, a proof that each of the nine drills can be completed, the attract director, dev mode, and an in-process two-copy AI Hub loopback); Playwright screenshots of every key screen (test/browser.mjs); the real AI Hub harness in all four configurations (test/hub.mjs: all pass); a TASK.md requirement checker run against the shipped HTML (test/taskcheck.mjs: 18 of 18 pass); the engine's tools/check.mjs\nAbout 19 issues: AI carriers almost never passed, then over-passed into 0-0 draws; three load-order crashes at boot (a d\n174 passed, 0 failed (15 suites, 19.2 s)\n18 of 18 TASK.md requirements pass; no game errors\nbrowser done\nidentical\n<title>Thugby Turbo</title>\nok players=2&auto=1\nok players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1\nok players=4&latency=150&jitter=50&loss=5&auto=1&scenario=host-leave\nok players=2&scenario=server&auto=1\nhub: all passed\nexit-interview.json parses\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha 1602684 Nov  4 08:04 ../ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html\n-rw-r--r-- 1 micha micha    7843 Nov  4 08:04 ../exit-interview.json","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01GaAjyTxSaCptcmoEjzxPBF","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi6YZNsDWYK3duPCiJz5","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A new checker, `test/taskcheck.mjs`, goes through TASK.md one requirement at a time against the shipped HTML itself: it runs the game straight from the file and prints evidence for each item. All 18 requirements pass, with no game errors.\n\nThe checker found one real bug and I fixed it. A key not bound to any action (X) didn't end the attract demo, although TASK.md says any input must. The demo now ends on any key or button, and the test suite checks that case too.\n\nThese all ran on the 08:04 build, and nothing was rebuilt afterwards:\n- **Tests:** 174 passed, 0 failed across 15 suites, including a playthrough of the whole Mega Cup and a completion proof for each of the 9 drills.\n- **AI Hub harness:** the real harness ran against a byte-identical copy of the deliverable (checked with `cmp`) and passed all 4 configurations.\n- **Browser:** the screenshot pass showed no errors.\n- **No stubs:** 0 matches for TODO, placeholder or stub text in the shipped game code.\n- **About & Credits:** the pages are regenerated from the current `exit-interview.json` and match the shipped HTML byte for byte. The JSON parses and every field is filled.\n\nTwo things that look like gaps:\n- **\"browser unknown\":** the Environment field uses TASK.md's own required wording, even though the tests ran in Playwright's Chromium.\n- **Set kicks:** they use a real behind-the-kicker camera, with the posts straight up the screen; the aim gauge is only a small overlay in the corner.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, training drills and multiplayer on one machine or online. A TV broadcast camera (the pitch left to right, seen from the stand at 40°), with High (55°) and Overhead options. Conversions and penalty goals cut to a behind-the-kicker camera with an inset aim and power gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (canvas 2D and Web Audio, 3 small patches); AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, unmodified; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file by a Node build script. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 are used for tests only. No other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel style. Each player is a dressed 3D character in their team kit, on a striped pitch with stands and a crowd in team colours. The oval ball, posts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and hazards are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's character rig, driven from match state, plus moves made for this game: diving tackles, flips when launched, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stunned stars, and a skeleton flash when hit by lightning.\n- **Audio:** All in code: seven songs (venue themes add drum and hype layers as play heats up), eight jingles, about 30 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2–4 players. The host runs the match; each client moves its own player locally while it's running free and the host accepts that position within limits. Clients draw 12 snapshots a second, with event bundles driving local commentary and effects. The game shows its own card, and finish, time-up, a player leaving and a host with no player of its own are all handled. Local play for 1–4 on one machine (controllers and two keyboard halves).\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command (`node test/run.mjs`) runs 15 suites, 174 checks, in about 25 s. It includes a scripted playthrough of the whole cup, a completion proof for each of the nine drills, the attract demo, dev mode, and a two-copy online test in one process.\n  - Playwright screenshots of every key screen (`test/browser.mjs`).\n  - The real AI Hub harness in all four configurations (`test/hub.mjs`, all pass).\n  - A TASK.md requirement checker run against the shipped HTML (`test/taskcheck.mjs`, 18 of 18 pass).\n  - The engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine handoff package (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md, the sports kit, parts of the engine source) and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files. No other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** unknown (no token or cost meter available); session time about 1 hour 10 minutes\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 19:\n   - The computer-controlled carriers almost never passed, then passed so much that matches ended 0–0.\n   - Three crashes at startup.\n   - A crash in the camera code.\n   - Kicks launched from where the ball used to be after a player was moved.\n   - Defenders caught drop goals straight off the boot.\n   - Penalty tries checked the wrong player.\n   - The final's giant boss was launched instead of needing two tacklers.\n   - Winning a scrum by collapsing it didn't count in the scrum drill.\n   - An online human kicker could stall the kick-off.\n   - A key not bound to any action didn't end the attract demo.\n   - Five layout bugs found in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four: the three startup crashes and the camera crash. All are fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup, together with the drill scripts that prove each of the nine drills can be completed.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed gives the same match. It never failed, because the simulation was deterministic from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** Running the game without a browser in exact fixed steps. Whole matches, the full cup and an online test with two copies run in seconds, and the same code draws in the browser.\n2. **Stuck or guessed wrong?** A few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key, which one of my tests didn't expect.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately rather than being queued.\n   - I had to find the right buffer to draw the skyline on before the pitch.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing I built:** The match simulation (rugby laws, set pieces, advantage, cards), the team AI, and one input layer that lets humans, the AI, tests, the attract demo and online clients all drive the same players.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 buffer. There is no built-in big menu or button-badge widget.\n5. **What I changed in the engine, and why:**\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font; they drew as \"?\".\n   - Made the two-players-on-one-keyboard key layout configurable per action.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box so each speaker can have a babble voice.\n6. **One thing I would change:** Make controller-glyph badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What I liked most:** It is concrete about what \"done\" means (attract mode, dev sliders, buttons that follow the last device, the touch-only screen, AI Hub), and the detailed plan meant building rather than guessing.\n2. **What was confusing:** The plan's file names (max effort, an earlier date) don't match TASK.md's. STAGE.md settles it (TASK.md wins), but it took a moment. The plan's very large scope also pulls against a \"feature-complete demo\" at medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Making the sport's AI look like rugby while keeping every system reachable in play, in the drills and in the attract demo, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What I would change:** Fill the plan's names in from TASK.md's values, and mark which plan items are optional polish and which are required features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1637,"cache_read_input_tokens":581102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1637},"output_tokens":743,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"16aa03f5-5c64-4aea-93d7-1d13a8d8c87c","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:07:18.464Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi6YYub9HGLLqSqjEAhG"}
