{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi6x184hC4RtDEC2f5LA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I finished **THUGBY TURBO**, an over-the-top arcade rugby sevens game, and every check passes on the final build (08:09:41). Nothing was changed after these runs:\n\n- **Requirements:** a script that runs the shipped HTML checks each TASK.md requirement and each gameplay feature separately; 44 of 44 pass. That covers the five power-ups, the four venue hazards, advantage, the hooter and golden point.\n- **Tests:** 174 passed, 0 failed across 15 suites, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup.\n- **AI Hub:** the real harness passed all four configurations on a byte-identical copy of the deliverable.\n- **Other checks:** no browser errors and no TODO, placeholder or stub text. `exit-interview.json` parses, every field is filled, and the About & Credits screen shows the same answers.\n- **Usage:** cost and tokens are unknown because no meter is available; the session ran about 1 hour 15 minutes.\n\nThe Environment line keeps TASK.md's required wording (\"browser unknown\"). The game itself is `ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html` in this folder.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs.\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, training drills, and multiplayer on one screen or online. The main camera is a TV broadcast view: the pitch runs left to right, seen from the stand at a 40° angle, with High (55°) and Overhead options. Conversions and penalty goals cut to a camera behind the kicker, with the posts straight up the screen and a small aim-and-power gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (canvas 2D and Web Audio, three small patches) and the AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, unmodified. Plain JavaScript, bundled by a Node build script into one self-contained HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium) are used for tests only; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's own PS1-era pixel style at 320×240, stretched to fill the screen. Each player is a dressed 3D character in team kit, on a striped textured pitch with stands and a bobbing crowd in team colours. The oval ball, goalposts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and venue hazards are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's own character rig, driven from match state (running gait, poses, built-in moves for passing, kicking, fending, tackling and spinning). Moves added for this game: diving tackles, mid-air flips after monster hits, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stun stars, and a skeleton flash when struck by lightning.\n- **Audio:** All written in code with the engine's chip synth:\n  - **Music:** seven songs, including four venue themes that bring in drum and hype layers as play heats up.\n  - **Sound:** eight short jingles and about 30 sound effects, including a crowd that follows the excitement.\n  - **Voices:** Animalese-style babble, one sound per letter, for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2–4 players. The host runs the match. Each client moves its own player immediately and reports where it is; the host accepts that within limits and decides everything else. Clients draw 12 updates a second from the host, and events from the host drive local commentary and effects. The game shows its own pre-match card and handles finish, time-up, players leaving, the end of a session, and a host with no player of its own. Locally, 1–4 players can share one machine (controllers and two keyboard halves).\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command (`node test/run.mjs`) runs 15 suites and 174 checks in about 25 seconds. It includes a scripted playthrough of the whole cup, a proof that each of the nine drills can be completed, the attract demo, dev mode, and an online test with two game copies in one process.\n  - Playwright screenshots of every key screen.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in all four configurations.\n  - A requirement checker run against the shipped HTML (44 of 44 pass).\n  - The engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff package (README, API.md, parts of AI_GUIDE.md, the sports kit, and the engine source around fonts, keyboard sharing, dialogs, menus, settings, the arcade module and saved data). The AI Hub `lib/hub` README, retrofit notes and harness. No other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** Cost and token count unknown (no token or cost meter is available in this session); session time about 1 hour 15 minutes (06:56 to about 08:10).\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 19:\n   - The computer-controlled carriers almost never passed, then passed so often that matches ended 0–0.\n   - Three crashes at startup, from code running in the wrong order.\n   - A crash in the camera code.\n   - Kicks launching from where the ball used to be after a player was moved.\n   - Defenders catching drop goals straight off the boot.\n   - Penalty tries checking the wrong player.\n   - The final's boss being launched instead of needing two tacklers.\n   - Winning a scrum by collapsing it not counting in the scrum drill.\n   - An online human kicker stalling the kick-off.\n   - A key not bound to any action failing to end the attract demo.\n   - Five layout problems seen in screenshots: the kick gauge, prompts overlapping commentary, a menu overflowing, the dev panel footer, and the title prompt.\n2. **How many would be considered critical / game breaking?** Four: the three startup crashes and the camera crash. All were fixed before shipping.\n3. **What was the most helpful test you ran?** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup (menus, story, training, four matches played by the autopilot, initials, victory and credits), together with the scripts that prove each of the nine drills can be completed.\n4. **What was the least helpful test you ran?** The check that the same seed always gives the same match. It never failed, because the simulation was deterministic from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about the engine?** It can run a game without a browser in exact fixed steps. Whole matches, the full cup and the two-copy online test run in seconds, and the same code draws in the browser.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** Yes, a few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key, which one test didn't expect.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately instead of being queued with other ground marks.\n   - I had to find the right drawing surface for the skyline so it sits behind the pitch.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n   - Controller symbols drawing as \"?\" was flagged in the plan and fixed.\n3. **What was the most important thing you had to build yourself?** The match simulation: the rugby laws and set pieces (rucks, scrums, lineouts, penalties, conversions, drop goals, advantage, cards), the team AI, and the input layer. That layer lets humans, the AI, the tests, the attract demo and online clients all drive the same players.\n4. **What was the hardest part about using the engine?** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 screen. The smallest readable text is the 5×7 font at normal size, so every screen needed careful layout, and there is no built-in large menu or controller-button badge.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** Three small patches:\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font, because they drew as \"?\".\n   - Let a game define its own key layout for two players sharing one keyboard.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box so every speaker can babble one sound per letter.\n6. **If you could change one thing about the engine, what would it be?** Ship controller-button badges and a scalable menu as standard widgets, so prompts automatically show PlayStation, Xbox, keyboard or mouse buttons for whatever device was used last.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most about this prompt?** It is concrete about what \"done\" means (attract mode, dev sliders, button symbols that follow the device, the touch-only message, AI Hub), and it came with a detailed plan, so the work was building rather than guessing.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan's file names (max effort, an earlier date) differ from TASK.md's. TASK.md wins, as STAGE.md says, but it took a moment to settle. \"Feature-complete demo\" and the plan's very large scope (every animation polished, several engine patches) also pull against each other at medium effort.\n3. **What was the hardest thing to do?** Making the rugby AI look like rugby (holding a shape, passing at the right moment) while keeping every set piece and arcade system reachable in normal play, the drills and the attract demo, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What would you change in this prompt?** Generate the plan's file names from TASK.md's values, and say plainly which plan items are optional polish and which are required features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2716,"cache_read_input_tokens":600438,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2716},"output_tokens":775,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"8f56b375-3caa-49bd-82bd-343f278fd747","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:12:41.405Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi6wzi1T68s4fCyjx7so"}
