{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi7E69cv1F7faB2DpuNP","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The game is finished and every check passes on the final build (`ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html` and `exit-interview.json`, both 08:13:25). Nothing was changed after these runs:\n\n- **TASK.md checklist:** 59 of 59 checks pass, no game errors. The checker runs the shipped HTML and prints each requirement with TASK.md's own line quoted beside the evidence. That covers Art Direction, Animation (17 distinct move states on the engine's own rig), Narrative tone, every mode screen (title, menu, cup, story, training, join, setup, records, about, credits, online card, results) and each gameplay feature, power-up and hazard, golden point included.\n- **Tests:** 174 passed, 0 failed across 15 suites, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup.\n- **AI Hub:** the real harness passes all four configurations on a byte-identical copy of the deliverable.\n- **Browser and code:** no browser errors, and no TODO, placeholder or stub text.\n- **exit-interview.json:** it parses and every field is filled. The About & Credits screen is rebuilt from it on every build and matches it byte for byte.\n\n**Answers to the points raised:**\n- **Kicker camera:** TASK.md names no kicker camera and asks for no polished animations; both came from PLAN.md. The set kick is a real camera move, a view rotated so the posts run straight up the screen, with a small aim gauge on top.\n- **Report sections:** the sections below follow the final `exit-interview.json`, which is what the shipped About screen displays.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a short story campaign, training drills, and multiplayer on one screen or online. The main camera is a TV broadcast view: the pitch runs left to right, seen from the stand at 40°, with High (55°) and Overhead options. Conversions and penalty goals cut to a camera behind the kicker (the view turned so the posts run straight up the screen), with a small aim and power gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (canvas 2D and Web Audio, three small patches) and the AI Hub Session Protocol v1 client 1.4.0, unmodified. Plain JavaScript, bundled by a Node build script into one self-contained HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 (Chromium) are used for tests only; no other libraries.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's own PS1-era pixel style at 320×240, stretched to fill the screen. Each player is a dressed 3D character in team kit, on a striped textured pitch with stands and a crowd in team colours. The oval ball, goalposts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and venue hazards are all drawn in code.\n- **Animation:** The engine's own character rig, driven from match state. Moves added for this game: superman dives, mid-air flips after monster hits, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stun stars and a skeleton flash after lightning.\n- **Audio:** All written in code with the engine's chip synth: seven songs (the venue themes add drum and hype layers as play heats up), eight jingles, about 30 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices for every speaker.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared-world sessions for 2–4 players. The host runs the match. Each client moves its own player immediately and the host accepts that position within limits. Clients draw 12 updates a second from the host, and events from the host drive local commentary and effects. The game shows its own pre-match card and handles finish, time-up, players leaving, the end of a session, and a host with no player of its own. Locally, 1–4 players share one machine.\n- **Testing:**\n  - One command (`node test/run.mjs`) runs 15 suites and 174 checks, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup, a completion proof for each of the nine drills, the attract demo, dev mode, and an online test with two game copies in one process.\n  - Playwright screenshots of every key screen.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in all four configurations.\n  - A TASK.md checker run against the shipped HTML (59 of 59 pass).\n  - The engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff package and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files; no other web research.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** Cost and token count unknown (no meter is available in this session); session time about 1 hour 20 minutes.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 19:\n   - The computer-controlled carriers almost never passed, then passed so often that matches ended 0–0.\n   - Three crashes at startup.\n   - A crash in the camera code.\n   - Kicks launching from an old ball position.\n   - Defenders catching drop goals straight off the boot.\n   - Penalty tries checking the wrong player.\n   - The final's boss being launched instead of needing two tacklers.\n   - A collapsed-scrum win not counting in the scrum drill.\n   - An online human kicker stalling the kick-off.\n   - A key not bound to any action failing to end the attract demo.\n   - Five layout problems seen in screenshots.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four: the three startup crashes and the camera crash. All are fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup, together with the scripts that prove each drill can be completed.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed gives the same match. It never failed, because the simulation was deterministic from the start.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** Running the game without a browser in exact fixed steps. Whole matches, the full cup and the two-copy online test run in seconds.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately instead of being queued.\n   - I had to find the right drawing surface for the skyline.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing you built yourself:** The match simulation (rugby laws, set pieces, advantage, cards), the team AI, and the input layer that lets humans, the AI, the tests, the attract demo and online clients drive the same players.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 screen, with no built-in large menu or controller-button badge.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font, because they drew as \"?\".\n   - Let a game define its own key layout for two players sharing a keyboard.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box so every speaker can have a babble voice.\n6. **One thing you would change:** Make controller-button badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** It defines \"done\" concretely, and it came with a detailed plan, so the work was building rather than guessing.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan's file names differ from TASK.md's. TASK.md wins, as STAGE.md says. The plan's very large scope also pulls against a feature-complete demo at medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Making the rugby AI look like rugby while keeping every system reachable in play, the drills and the attract demo, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What would you change?** Generate the plan's names from TASK.md's values, and say plainly which plan items are optional polish and which are required features."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2873,"cache_read_input_tokens":609526,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2873},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"fad85cea-126d-42eb-980d-e3f20074113a","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:16:15.137Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi7E5oYWCLBDZPopRXWa"}
