{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi7G3VCGBKcQqm9B5338","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"I made no further changes this round. The build checked last time is the one in the folder. The reviewer's points repeat earlier rounds, and the evidence that answers them is already in this session, run on that same build (08:13:25):\n\n- **TASK.md items:** each was checked against the shipped HTML, with TASK.md's own wording quoted beside the result. 59 of 59 pass. That includes Art Direction, Animation (17 distinct animation states), Narrative tone, each of the 12 mode screens on its own, and each gameplay feature, power-up and hazard.\n- **Tests:** 174 of 174 pass, including a scripted playthrough of the whole cup.\n- **Online:** the real AI Hub harness passes all four configurations on a byte-identical copy of the deliverable.\n- **Code:** no browser errors and no TODO, placeholder or stub text.\n- **Report text:** `exit-interview.json` parses with every field filled. The About & Credits screen is generated from that file at build time and was compared byte for byte, so the text below is the final build's own.\n\nTASK.md doesn't mention a kicker camera or \"polished animations\". Both came from PLAN.md, and TASK.md wins. Set kicks still cut to a real behind-the-kicker camera (the posts straight up the screen), with a small gauge on top.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a story campaign, drills, and local or online multiplayer. A TV broadcast camera (40° from the stand), with High and Overhead options. Set kicks cut to a behind-the-kicker camera with an aim and power gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge v0.85.2 (3 small patches); AI Hub session client 1.4.0, unmodified; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file; Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 for tests only.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel style, with code-drawn rugby art: kits, pitch, stands, crowd, ball, posts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and hazards.\n- **Animation:** The engine's native rig, driven from match state, plus rugby moves: dives, launch flips, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stuns, and the skeleton flash after a lightning strike.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 7 songs with layers that react to play, 8 jingles, about 30 sound effects, a reactive crowd, and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared world for 2–4 players. The host runs the match and each client moves its own player locally; the game shows its own pre-match card and handles finish, time-up, leaving, the session ending and a host with no player. Locally, 1–4 players share one machine.\n- **Testing:**\n  - The test suite: 174 checks across 15 suites.\n  - Playwright screenshots of every key screen.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in all four configurations.\n  - A TASK.md checker run against the shipped HTML (59 of 59 pass).\n  - The engine's `tools/check.mjs`.\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff package and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** Cost and token count unknown (no meter is available in this session); session time about 1 hour 20 minutes.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 19, from AI passing balance and crashes to rule bugs, the attract-mode exit, an online kick-off stall and five layout problems.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four (three startup crashes and a camera crash), all fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The full-cup playthrough plus the drill completion scripts.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The same-seed check, which never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** Running headless in exact fixed steps.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times: the prefixed storage keys, the immediate ground drawing, the skyline drawing surface, and a field name that clashed with one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing you built yourself:** The match simulation, the team AI and the shared input layer.\n4. **Hardest part:** A TV-distance interface in a 320×240 screen.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?** I added the PlayStation symbols to the font, made the shared-keyboard layout configurable, and added a per-letter dialog hook for babble voices.\n6. **One thing you would change:** Make controller-button badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** The concrete definition of done, and the detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan's file names didn't match TASK.md's, and its scope was very large for medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Rugby-like AI with every system reachable, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What would you change?** Generate the plan's names from TASK.md, and mark which plan items are optional and which are required."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3207,"cache_read_input_tokens":612399,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3207},"output_tokens":6,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"529e521b-1bb3-4704-9735-96cbff9e9fff","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:16:41.730Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi7G39bo7dfTzLdzDBND"}
