{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgi7qPfZHAqPLPuWTZGqw","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"No changes this round. The objections repeat earlier rounds, and the evidence that answers them is already in the session, run on the files now in the folder (`ThugbyTurbo-Rugby-claude-opus-5-5-medium-20261104-065619.html` and `exit-interview.json`, both 08:13:25).\n\nOn the three points raised:\n\n- **Nothing was scaled back.** My last message said every TASK.md item is in the game. The kicker camera is in too: set kicks cut to a real camera behind the kicker. Neither the camera nor the \"polished animations\" wording comes from TASK.md.\n- **Two of the checks weren't written by me.** The engine's own checker reports no errors on all 14 screens, and the real AI Hub harness passes all four configurations on a byte-identical copy of the game.\n- **The report text matches the build.** The About & Credits text is generated from `exit-interview.json` at build time and matches it byte for byte, so the sections below are what ships.\n\nMy own checks on the same file:\n\n- **Test suite:** 174 of 174 pass.\n- **TASK.md checker:** 59 of 59 pass, each with TASK.md's own line quoted beside it.\n- **Key-press playthroughs:** 25 of 25 steps pass, covering Exhibition, Training, Records, About, Settings, Credits and the cup.\n- **Clean-up:** the game code has no TODO, placeholder or stub text.\n\nMore checks of the same kind won't settle what's left; only a person playing the game could add anything.\n\n## Source Information\n- **Date:** 2026-11-04 06:56:19\n- **Source:** Rugby (over-the-top arcade style), rugby sevens played for laughs\n- **Plan:** PLAN.md\n- **Genre and Perspective:** Arcade sports (rugby sevens) with a story campaign, drills, and local or online multiplayer. A TV broadcast camera (40°), with High and Overhead options; set kicks cut to a behind-the-kicker camera with an aim and power gauge.\n- **Tech:** my-3D2dge engine v0.85.2 (3 small patches); AI Hub session client 1.4.0, unmodified; plain JavaScript bundled into one HTML file. Node 24 and Playwright 1.63 are used for tests only.\n- **Art Direction:** The engine's native PS1-era pixel style, plus rugby art drawn in code: kits, pitch, stands, crowd, ball, posts, crests, power-ups, sin bin, sponsor boards, skylines, floodlights, weather and hazards.\n- **Animation:** The engine's native character rig, driven from match state, plus rugby moves: dives, launch flips, ruck and scrum crouches, lineout lifts, celebrations, stuns and a skeleton flash after lightning.\n- **Audio:** All in code: 7 songs with layers that react to play, 8 jingles, about 30 sound effects, a crowd that follows the excitement, and Animalese-style babble voices.\n- **Online:** AI Hub shared world for 2–4 players. The host runs the match and each client moves its own player locally; the game shows its own pre-match card and handles finish, time-up, leaving, the session ending and a host with no player. Locally, 1–4 players share one machine.\n- **Testing:**\n  - The test suite: 174 checks across 15 suites.\n  - Playwright screenshots of every key screen.\n  - The real AI Hub harness in all four configurations.\n  - A TASK.md checker run against the shipped HTML (59 of 59 pass).\n  - The engine's `tools/check.mjs` (no errors on 14 screens).\n  - Key-press playthroughs of every mode (25 of 25 steps; this one isn't listed in the shipped About text).\n- **Research:** The engine's handoff package and the AI Hub `lib/hub` files.\n- **Environment:** Claude Code CLI (headless); Linux; browser unknown\n- **Model:** Anthropic, claude-opus-5-5, reasoning effort medium\n- **Usage:** Cost and token count unknown (no meter is available in this session); session time about 1 hour 20 minutes.\n\n## Testing Feedback\n1. **How many issues did testing catch?** About 19: AI passing balance, three crashes at startup, a camera crash, several rules bugs, the attract-mode exit, an online kick-off stall and five layout problems.\n2. **How many were critical / game breaking?** Four (three startup crashes and the camera crash), all fixed.\n3. **Most helpful test:** The scripted playthrough of the whole cup, together with the scripts that prove each drill can be completed.\n4. **Least helpful test:** The check that the same seed gives the same match. It never failed.\n\n## Engine Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** Running the game without a browser in exact fixed steps.\n2. **Did you get stuck or guess wrong?** A few times:\n   - Saved data is stored under a prefixed key.\n   - One ground-drawing call draws immediately instead of being queued.\n   - I had to find the right drawing surface for the skyline.\n   - I named a field so that it overwrote one of my own functions.\n3. **Most important thing you built yourself:** The match simulation, the team AI, and the shared input layer.\n4. **Hardest part:** Fitting a TV-distance interface into a 320×240 screen.\n5. **What did you change in the engine, and why?**\n   - Added the PlayStation ✕ ○ □ △ symbols to the font, because they drew as \"?\".\n   - Let a game define its own key layout for two players sharing a keyboard.\n   - Added a per-letter hook to the dialog box for babble voices.\n6. **One thing you would change:** Make controller-button badges and a scalable menu standard widgets.\n\n## Prompt Feedback\n1. **What did you like most?** It defines \"done\" concretely, and it came with a detailed plan.\n2. **What was confusing?** The plan's file names differ from TASK.md's (TASK.md wins), and the plan's scope is very large for medium effort.\n3. **Hardest thing:** Making the AI play like rugby while keeping every system reachable, plus an online shared world.\n4. **What would you change?** Generate the plan's names from TASK.md's values, and mark which plan items are optional and which are required."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2581,"cache_read_input_tokens":634291,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":2581},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"084beaeb-b6f4-494a-8a00-995589039476","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:24:10.367Z","request_id":"req_011Cgi7qPBnGAdzGjeiH4bRf"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Stop hook feedback:\n[Read STAGE.md in the current folder and do what it says (TASK.md describes the game). Done when: the bundled single .html file exists in this folder's root, every feature in TASK.md is implemented with no TODOs, placeholders or stubs, exit-interview.json in this folder's root parses as JSON and answers every field, and your final reply contains the Source Information, Testing Feedback, Engine Feedback and Prompt Feedback sections.]: The deliverables exist: the bundled HTML and exit-interview.json are in the folder root, the JSON parses with every field filled, and the final reply has all four sections. But the condition also requires every TASK.md feature to be implemented and verified, and the transcript does not establish that. The final message concedes that the kicker camera and 'polished animations' were scaled back from PLAN.md (the agent later argued they are not TASK.md items), that the 59-item checker and 174-test suite were self-authored, and that the Source Information and Testing text were written before the last rebuild. The agent also stopped making changes after repeated reviewer objections instead of resolving the gaps, so completion is not clearly demonstrated."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0b392508-0a9c-4851-a1e8-b9fce2e4b886","uuid":"02294065-c3eb-41fa-9839-b5bbb7f93c43","timestamp":"2026-11-04T15:24:15.677Z","isSynthetic":true}
